export const PROTOCOL_TEXT: Record<string, string> = {
  "practices/confidentiality-consent-refusal": "6 Patient Confidentiality: Patient is defined as an individual who requests or has the potential of needing medical evaluation and/or treatment. 104-191 Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 regulates the protection of information obtained when responding, evaluating, treating, and transporting patients. It is imperative that we maintain the confidence of patient information and ensure its security. Mulvane EMS prohibits the release of any patient information to anyone outside the organization unless required for purposes of treatment, payment, or health care operations. Every effort should be made to ensure patient information is not left open in public view or unattended. Legal Consent: \u2022 Informed Consent: Originates from the legal and ethical right the patient has to direct what happens to his/her body. A conscious patient must be told in a manner he/she can understand, the nature and extent of the procedure to be performed and the possible risks involved. If consent is refused by a conscious and mentally competent adult for any treatment procedure following explanation, that person cannot be treated. \u2022 Implied Consent: Happens when you are unable to communicate with the patient. Most commonly, this is because the patient is unconscious. When a patient cannot express his or her wishes, there is an assumption that he/she would ask to be treated at the scene and transported to a medical facility where emergency life-saving treatment may be given. \u2022 Minor Consent: The age of majority in Kansas is 18 years of age. To administer care to a minor requires parental or guardian consent (either in person or by telephone). If a responsible person is not available, emergency treatment to sustain life and alleviate pain and suffering may be undertaken without consent. Refusal of Treatment: It is the policy of Mulvane EMS to give the competent adult patient the right to refuse examination, treatment and/or transport for any illness or injury. The following guidelines must be followed to allow a patient to be released: \u2022 The patient MUST be competent and rational to make the decision to refuse service. \u2022 The patient MUST be 18 years of age or have written documentation of emancipation. \u2022 The patient must be aware of complications that could arise from the decision to not be transported. 7 If the patient is able to answer basic questions (ie. age/name/etc.) but still seems confused or altered or if there is continued concern regarding the patient\u2019s decision-making capability, administer the Quick Confusion Scale to aid in determination of transport necessity. \u2756 Note: If the patient is not competent to make the decision and refuses treatment and transport, the next of kin should be notified to persuade the patient. If necessary contact the Police Department to place the patient under protective custody to provide legal treatment and transport. \u2756 Note: If the patient is combative the safety of the crew is paramount and restraints should be used, refer to restraint procedure for guidelines.",
  "practices/physician-on-scene-dnr": "8 Procedure when a Physician is on the Scene: For situations in which a physician is on the scene, the following procedure should be followed; such situations are divided into two types for the purpose of this procedure. Routine, Normal Situations \u2022 Conduct yourself in a professional demeanor and respectful attitude at all times. \u2022 Inquire if the physician is the patient\u2019s regular physician. If so, consult the physician as to the patient\u2019s history, prior treatment, etc. \u2022 Advise the physician that we have medical standing protocols established by our Medical Director and request that we are allowed to continue under these standing orders. \u2022 Follow any reasonable request by the physician. \u2022 Verbal orders received from in-town physicians and/or practitioners may be followed without them riding in to the hospital with the proper documentation. Problem or Crisis Situation \u2022 Follow the steps outlined above. \u2022 If the physician prefers to assume responsibility for the patient, the following requirements must be satisfied. o The Physician must provide proof of Kansas licensure. o The Physician must accompany the patient to the medical facility in our unit. o The physician must initiate any procedure that is contrary to our protocols. \u2022 If, in the opinion of the charge technician, the patient\u2019s well-being is endangered or if the physician does not agree to our procedure in its entirety, the receiving Emergency Room Physician shall be contacted. Do Not Resuscitate (DNR) Directives and Orders: The following guidelines will help when a DNR situation is encountered. \u2022 If the care provider believes a DNR order exists, attempt to locate the order while continuing with appropriate care. o If a valid DNR, Directive, or Order is produced at any point during a resuscitative effort, all resuscitative efforts should be stopped. o A Do Not Resuscitate (DNR) Directive or Order may be honored if ANY of the following conditions are met: \u27a2 A valid pre-hospital directive or authorization by an attorney in fact named in a valid Durable Power of Attorney for Health Care Decisions. \u27a2 A physician\u2019s order to \u201cDo Not Resuscitate\u201d. \u27a2 A DNR Medic Alert identifier (bracelet or necklace). Terminally ill patients who have a Living Will or TPOPP/POLST (Transportable Physician Orders for Patient Preferences/Physician Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment) may be treated per the provisions of these documents instead of Mulvane EMS Standing Orders or Protocols. In the absence of one of these valid documents, terminally ill patients may be transported without oxygen, IV fluids or medications at the request of a lucid patient or an attorney in fact named in a valid Durable Power of Attorney for Health Care Decisions.",
  "practices/infection-control": "9 Infection Control and Exposures: It is everyone\u2019s duty to protect themselves, their crew, and the patients from infection and exposures. By simple measures all personnel and patients can be exposed to hazards. The following are precautions to help eliminate those exposures. \u2022 At the beginning of every shift the crew will decontaminate the truck. This is to include the entire back and front passenger compartments and equipment. \u2022 Immediately after every call: o Linens will be replaced. o Equipment wiped down. o Counters wiped down. \u2022 Once each month each truck will have the cabinets emptied and thoroughly cleaned. This is to include the back, outside compartments, and the front passenger compartment. \u2022 All sharps containers should be used for needles and sharp objects only. Do not fill past the recommended fill height. Any full containers will be properly disposed of and replaced with new ones. \u2022 Do not eat, drink, apply makeup, or smoke as these will increase the risk of an exposure. \u2022 Anytime patient contact is made, the provider should take universal precautions to reduce the risk of exposures. These key tips will help minimize exposures: o Wear non-latex gloves when there is a possibility of contact with any bodily fluids. o Wear eye protection and gown whenever there is a splash hazard. o Washing hands after every call is the easiest way to decrease exposure. o During vehicle extrication the crew will wear the provided protective clothing inside the work area established by the fire department. o A safety vest will be worn on all scenes on a roadway. \u2022 If an exposure occurs contact one of the following: o EMS Lieutenant o EMS Captain o Public Safety Chief \u2022 If the exposure is in the eyes or mucous membrane the area should be flushed for at least 15 minutes. All other exposures should be washed with water and a non-abrasive antibacterial soap. Lastly, an exposure report will be filled out and will remain on file for no less than 40 years.",
  "practices/quality-assurance": "10 Quality Assurance: The role of Quality Assurance is to evaluate the quality of care provided to patients requesting service from Mulvane EMS. Quality Assurance is comprised of multiple quality control activities. These activities exist within our organization to provide consistent quality care, and to evaluate existing policies or procedures. Moreover, quality control activities exist to improve the quality of care provided and enhance the EMS system\u2019s responsiveness to the citizens and visitors of Mulvane and the surrounding area. Quality Assurance is a structured and functional review of quality control activities and provides feedback to the involved individuals, which can effectuate desirable change. The approval of these Protocols and Standing Orders is contingent on the continued operation of a Quality Assurance Program and periodic (quarterly) reporting of the results of the process must be made to the Medical Director of Mulvane EMS. Definitions and Components \u2022 Protocol: Establishes the standards to be achieved under specific circumstances within the EMS system. Guidelines contained herein provide conditions, restrictions, or requirements when accomplishing medical procedures or treatment regimes. Protocols are useful in teaching and provide a standard by which care provided can be reviewed by the service Medical Director. \u2022 Standing Order: Provides legal authority to carry out procedures on behalf of the physician(s) who has signed them. Additionally, they authorize treatment before radio contact is made. Usually these orders provide treatment of time urgent situations, i.e. cardiac arrest. \u2022 On-Line Medical Control: Provides direct real-time physician to pre-hospital provider communication. Once contact is made, the pre-hospital provider becomes an agent of the on-line physician and carries out orders given. \u2022 Off-Line Medical Control: Provides medical direction prior to or upon review of the actual real time emergency. Included in off-line medical control are: Protocols, Standing Orders and Call Critiques. Compliance by EMS personnel with Protocol and Standing Orders is evaluated by an audit and review process which is supervised by the Medical Director. \u2022 Medical Director: A person licensed to practice medicine and surgery in the State of Kansas. The Medical Director is responsible for Educational Standards, Protocol Development, Critique, Planning and Quality Assurance. Active involvement in off-line medical control is essential. Procedures for Quality Control \u2022 Off-Line Activities: o Chart Review: Charts written by field personnel are audited to evaluate compliance with departmental Protocol and Standing Orders. \u2022 On-Line Activities: o Medical Control: Each time medical orders, beyond Standing Orders are anticipated, a medical control physician is to be contacted. The seven local medical control facilities are: Via-Christi St. Francis Regional Medical Center Via-Christi St. Joseph Regional Medical Center Via-Christ Wellington ER Wesley Medical Center Wesley Woodlawn Hospital Wesley Derby Rock Regional Medical Center Robert J Dole Veteran\u2019s Hospital These constitute the majority of receiving facilities for Mulvane emergency patients. The medical control physicians should be contacted during unusual occurrences or situations that require special attention. These orders require the following stipulations to be documented on the chart by the technician: The name of the Physician that gave the order; The verbatim content of the \u201cVerbal Order.\u201d; The signature of the Physician giving the order. 11 Feedback and Evaluation: Communication will be made to field personnel when a Protocol or Standing Order violation is suspected during the peer review process. Frequently, the communication will provide sufficient information to eliminate or confirm a suspicion. If Protocol and/or Standing Order violations are unclear, situations are referred to the Medical Director for determination. If a violation exists, remedial training and/or disciplinary action may be undertaken.",
  "triage-transport/patient-transport-destination": "13 Patient Transport/Destination: The following guidelines provide general rules for the establishment of hospital destinations for patients transported by Mulvane EMS. Non-routine factors, such as poor road or weather conditions, diversion status of hospitals, number of patients requiring transport, etc., may alter how these guidelines are followed. Approved Hospital Destinations \u2022 Approved receiving hospital destinations for Mulvane EMS are: \u2022 Rock Regional Hospital \u2022 Wesley Derby ER \u2022 Wesley Main \u2022 Wesley Woodlawn \u2022 Via Christi St. Joseph \u2022 Via Christi St. Francis \u2022 Via Christi Wellington (Code Green and Orange only) Emergency Transports \u2022 Non-Critical Patients: (Code Green) will be transported to either Rock Regional Hospital, Wesley Derby ER and Via Christi Wellington ER. The exception is VA patients, institutional calls or pre-arranged transfers. \u2022 Workman\u2019s Compensation situations may allow the employer to determine hospital destination. \u2022 Patients in police custody may be transported to a destination selected by the arresting officer. \u2022 Code Orange Patients: Should be transported to the area hospital of the patient\u2019s preference unless a legal guardian, spouse, or law enforcement officer requests an alternate facility. Any ER can assess/treat patients with psychiatric/mental health complaints. \u2022 Emergency Critical Patients: (Code Red or Blue) will be transported to the appropriate facility of the patient preference or as directed by a spouse or legal guardian, VA excluded. If a patient cannot or will not make such a choice, transport will be made to the closest appropriate Wichita hospital facility; VA excluded. \u2022 Deceased Patients (Code Black): Will not be routinely transported by EMS. Only following a \u201ccoroner\u2019s release\u201d will a patient be moved or transported to the morgue of the coroner\u2019s choice. Normally, transport of deceased patients is requested by law enforcement personnel at the scene when the body is in public view. \u2022 Isolated Carbon Monoxide Poisonings: Transport to Wesley Medical Center for Hyperbaric Oxygen (HBO) treatment. If the etiology is uncertain, follow the most appropriate transport guideline. Burn Alert Patients: \u2022 Burn Alert Patients: Transport only to Via Christi St. Francis. \u2756 Note: Code Blue Burned OB (with or without trauma) shall be transported to Wesley Medical Center. STEMI Alert Patients: \u2022 STEMI Alerts: Transport will be to Wesley Medical Center, Via Christi St. Francis, or Kansas Medical Center. \u2022 Code Blue/Post Code Blue: Transport only to Via-Christi St. Francis or Wesley Medical Center.",
  "triage-transport/type-1-assist": "14 Stroke Alert Patients: \u2022 Stroke Alert Patients (Code Red): Transport only to Via Christi St. Francis or Wesley Medical Center. Trauma Alert Patients: \u2022 General: o EMS personnel may deviate from these protocols if conditions exist that would significantly affect transport time to the defined trauma center. o No more than four (4) trauma alert patients from a single incident will be transported to the same Trauma Center. o Obstetrical patients at or beyond 20 weeks gestation meeting any of the field triage criteria for a Code Yellow Rapid Fetal assessment or a Code Red or Code Blue Trauma Alert, will be transported to Wesley Medical Center, which must be notified they will be receiving an obstetrical trauma patient. \u2022 Trauma Alert Patients: Transport will be patient preference or to the closest Level I Trauma Center (Via Christi St. Francis or Wesley Medical Center). \u2022 Pediatric Trauma: Wesley Medical Center ONLY. Non-Emergency Transport \u2022 Routine transfer patients will be transported to any location the patient, patient\u2019s spouse, or legal guardian requests. If the patient\u2019s condition deteriorates during transport use the most appropriate emergency transport guide. Type 1 Assists: \u2022 BLS Crew: Sedgwick County EMS will be contacted for ALS assistance for any patient with a triage of Code Yellow, Code Red, or Code Blue. The intercept may take place at the scene or a safe place along the roadway. Procedurally, the paramedic should board our ambulance and treat the patient while they are en route to the receiving facility. It may be necessary to transfer the patient to the Type I unit; however, this should be limited to situations where lack of equipment, communication or vehicular problems exist with the Type II unit.",
  "triage-transport/triage-guidelines": "15 Guidelines for Patient Triage: Code Green: (Non-Serious) \u2022 No appearance of life-threatening injury or illness. \u2022 Vital signs within normal limits. \u2022 Closed fractures, minor lacerations, minor illness, isolated hip fracture (with stable vital signs). \u2022 Patient is alert and oriented. \u2022 Patients who are stable and are anticipated to remain stable. Code Yellow: (Serious) \u2022 Urgent Patients. \u2022 Any Patient given any medication. \u2022 Patient requires paramedic evaluation. \u2022 IV and/or monitoring have been established. \u2022 V or above on the AVPU scale. \u2022 Initially stable but may deteriorate. Medical Trauma CVA with stable vital signs (Outside of Stroke Alert) Code Yellow Trauma Alerts Cardiac/Chest Pain with stable vitals Code Yellow Rapid Fetal Assessment Respiratory Disease/Distress patient Medication intervention required Overdoses with stable vital signs and LOC of A or V Alterations in vital signs OB patients with impending delivery or field delivery without anticipated complications. Diabetic with LOC of V (AVPU) Other medical emergencies with anticipated alterations of vital signs. Postictal Seizures Newborn APGAR \u2265 8 Reactive Airway Disease requiring medication intervention Code Red: (Critical) \u2022 Requires ALS intervention \u2022 Immediate Patients \u2022 Unstable Vital Signs Medical Trauma CVA/TIA within criteria (onset <6hr or wake-up stroke) Code Red Trauma Alerts LOC of P or U (AVPU) Code Red Burn Alert Hemodynamically unstable cardiac patients (Acute MI on 12 lead) Dysrhythmias or Cardiogenic Shock OB with complications Newborn with APGAR of < 7 Overdose with LOC of P or U or unstable vital signs Code Red Sepsis Alert 16 Code Blue: (Critical) \u2022 Requires ALS \u2022 Cardiac or Respiratory arrest Code Orange: (Psychiatric) \u2022 Patient demonstrates unusual, bizarre or socially aberrant action, ideas or moods and represents a threat to themselves or others. May require law enforcement. \u2022 No medical/trauma issue/complaint is present (Must address/triage according to medical/trauma issue first) Code Black: (Dead on Scene) \u2022 Decapitation \u2022 Blunt trauma without vital signs upon arrival (After performing basic airway maneuvers and bilateral needle chest decompression; it is at the discretion of the Paramedic to run the full cardiac arrest protocol.) \u2022 Incineration \u2022 Decomposition \u2022 Rigor Mortis \u2022 Dependent Lividity \u2022 DNR Order \u2022 Penetrating head trauma without vital signs \u2022 Adult Only! Prolonged field resuscitation with an asystole or PEA rhythm 20 minutes or greater (in the absence of hypothermia) * In rare circumstances ie body in public view the code black may be transported to facility of Sumner County Coroner\u2019s choice. *If Code Black is in Sedgwick county do not move body unless requested by law enforcement or Sedgwick County Medical Examiner\u2019s Office.",
  "triage-transport/general-assessment-protocol": "17 General Assessment: The General Assessment procedure will be used for all patients and should be completed for every patient that the provider encounters. Patient Definition \u2022 Patient Criteria: An assessment, history, and offer of transport will be performed and documented. o Request for EMS by him/herself. o Apparent illness or injury. o Has a Chief Complaint. o Vital signs are obtained. (Exception: walk-in request for blood pressure check without complaint of illness or injury.) \u2022 No Patient Criteria: o Accidental medical alarm activation. o No person on scene. o MVC victim with no apparent injury or complaint. \u2022 Citizen Assist Criteria: o Request for assistance with medical equipment who has no acute illness, injury, or complaint. (Example: changing an oxygen cylinder.) o Lift assistance with no injury or illness causing the fall, no complaint of injury, and no vital signs assessed. Scene Size-up \u2022 Scene Safety: Paramount. All providers will make a scene assessment upon arrival to determine any unsafe scene or patient. If the scene is unsafe, providers will standby until the scene is secured by proper personnel. \u2022 Body Substance Isolation (BSI): All personnel will take proper precautionary measures when making contact with patients (i.e. gloves, glasses, gowns, and masks.) Providers should also wash hands after calls, as hand washing is one of the best ways to prevent spreading diseases. \u2022 Mechanism of injury (MOI) / Nature of illness (NOI): MOI is the event that caused the injury. On-scene size up should indicate potential injuries. NOI is the patient\u2019s reason/chief complaint for calling EMS. \u2022 Number of patients and triage: It is crucial that the number of patients is ascertained. If there are more than two patients, additional resources may be needed and/or a need to establish an Incident Command System. Triage is the condition of the patient. \u2022 Consider additional resources: Additional resources should be requested as needed. 18 Initial Assessment Complete as indicated. Scene time should be kept to a minimum. A Rapid Trauma Assessment and a detailed physical exam should be done on traumas. A focused history should be done on medicals. \u2022 Determine level of consciousness. \u2022 Assess circulation, airway, and breathing. \u2022 Perform a Quick head-to-toe assessment. \u2022 Identify any life-threatening injuries or illness and treat. \u2022 Obtain and record vital signs, including: peripheral pulse (rate, regularity and quality), blood pressure, respirations (character and rate), skin (condition, temperature and color), breath sounds, motor function and pupils. This should be done a minimum of every fifteen (15) minutes or more frequently as indicated by patient condition. \u2022 Obtain a SAMPLE history. Signs/symptoms, Allergies, Medications, Prior medical history, Last oral intake, Events leading up to, and Interventions. This can be information from the patient, family and/or bystanders. Detailed Physical Exam A Detailed Physical Exam should be completed on all trauma patients. When performing a detailed physical exam, utilize DCAP-BTLS. Deformities, Contusions, Abrasions, Punctures/Penetrations, Burns, Tenderness, Lacerations, and Swelling. A detailed physical exam should examine the following: \u2022 Head, Neck, and C-Spine: Skull, face, eyes, mouth, ears, nose, and throat \u2022 Chest: Breath sounds (Rate, rhythm, and quality), crepitus, and bruising \u2022 Abdomen: Soft, firm, tender, distended, and guarding \u2022 Pelvis: Stability, pain, and tenderness \u2022 Extremities: Distal circulation, sensation, and motor \u2022 Back: Stability and incontinence Focused History When warranted a focused history should be performed and included as a part of the SAMPLE history. OPQRSTI will assist with cardiac assessments. OPQRSTI is as follows: \u2022 Onset: When did it start? \u2022 Provokes: What makes it better or worse? \u2022 Quality: What does the pain feel like? \u2022 Radiates: Does it go anywhere else on your body? \u2022 Severity: On a scale of 1-10, rate the pain? \u2022 Timing: Is pain constant or does it come and go? \u2022 Interventions: What have they done or taken to help? \u2756 Note: If the patient is unresponsive a rapid assessment should be performed. Field Treatments Treat appropriately in order of priority. 19 Oxygen Administration Oxygen administration may be applied by all levels of providers. Based on patient need and/or protocol, supplemental oxygen will be administered to maintain a saturation level above 94% with the appropriate delivery device determined by the technician. Blood Glucose Blood glucose will be assessed on all patients that have an altered level of consciousness, suspected hyper/hypoglycemia, unknown etiology, ETOH suspicion, overdoses, seizures and at the discretion of the technician. All patients that are being treated for diabetic emergencies need to have blood glucose levels taken every 15 minutes or whenever a treatment is done. Communications A patient report will be given to the hospital as early as possible during transport using the following format: \u2022 Identify ambulance service, level of care (ALS/BLS), and yourself (John Doe, EMT). \u2022 Patient\u2019s age, sex, vital signs, LOC, Doctor and triage. \u2022 Patient\u2019s chief complaint and physical assessment findings. \u2022 Pertinent medical history. \u2022 Mechanism of injury (if applicable), or events preceding condition or problem. \u2022 Treatment given, patients response, and present status. \u2022 Estimated time of arrival (ETA) at hospital. \u2022 Any other pertinent information requested. \u2022 Advise emergency department of changes occurring in patient status during transport. Upon arrival, give a verbal report to the receiving nurse or physician. \u2022 If radio communication with Sedgwick Co. 800MHz system is unavailable contact the receiving facility via cellular phone. All calls to facilities where orders are to be received should be placed through a dispatch center for recorded record of the orders. \u2022 Run reports should be written and locked within two hours of returning to the station.",
  "triage-transport/inter-facility-transfer": "20 Inter-Facility Transfers: Intra-facility transfer requests will be approved on an individual basis dependent upon availability of staff, equipment, and the urgency of the transfer. The patient will be transported to the requested facility. \u2022 Primary Survey: CAB \u2022 Secondary Survey o Head to Toe survey o Obtain pertinent medical history. o Obtain reason for transfer. o Obtain paperwork for receiving facility. \u2022 Administer oxygen if indicated. \u2022 Monitor vitals every 10-15 minutes. \u2022 Monitor I.V. Solutions o EMT\u2019s may monitor the following IV Solutions: \u27a2 D5W \u27a2 D10W \u27a2 D5LR \u27a2 Lactated Ringers (LR) \u27a2 0.9% NaCL (NS) \u27a2 0.45% NaCL \u27a2 0.2% NaCl \u2022 Place patient in a position of comfort. \u2022 Re-evaluate patient condition for changing status. \u2022 Treat patient appropriately for underlying causes. \u2022 Follow treatment orders from sending facility and request to use standing orders for situations outside of current orders.",
  "triage-transport/burn-alert-criteria": "21 Alert Criteria: All alerts must also have a complete radio report given to the receiving hospital during transport. Burn Alert Criteria: Burns All burns will be assessed using the \u201cRule of Nines\u201d to determine the total burn surface area (TBSA) of the patient and only partial (2nd degree) and full thickness (3rd degree) burns will determine burn alert codes. All burn patients will be transported to Via Christi St. Francis (VCSF) except OB. All obstetrical burn patients will be transported to Wesley Medical Center first before being transferred to Via Christi St. Francis. Burn Alert Notification All burn alerts need to be communicated with Dispatch as soon as possible and include the following: \u2022 Triage \u2022 Age \u2022 Gender \u2022 GCS \u2022 Total Burn Surface Area, degree of burn, and location on patient\u2019s body. \u2022 Type of burn (thermal, chemical, electrical, inhalation). \u2022 Any associated trauma. \u2022 Destination. \u2022 ETA. Code Red Burn Alert Criteria Any patient with any one of the following meets Code Red Burn alert: \u2022 10% or greater Total Body Surface Area (TBSA) with partial thickness and/or full thickness burns. \u2022 Airway and/or respiratory difficulties associated with inhalation of smoke (soot in nares/airway), chemicals, or thermal exposures. \u2022 440 volt or greater electrical burns. \u2022 Lightning strikes. \u2022 Circumferential burns to head, neck, and torso. \u2022 Chemical burns to head, neck, torso, and face. \u2022 Potential for inhalation injuries. \u2022 Chemical burns to the hands, arms, legs, and feet. \u2022 Circumferential burns to the hands, arms, legs, and feet. \u2022 Burns to the peritoneal area, genitalia, and major joints. 22 Burn Alert Criteria: Burns All burns will be assessed using the \u201cRule of Nines\u201d to determine the total burn surface area (TBSA) of the patient and only partial (2nd degree) and full thickness (3rd degree) burns will determine burn alert codes. Rule of Nines Palmar Method \u2013 Using the patient\u2019s palm to represent 1% of body surface area offers a quick estimation of the area burned. The Rule of Nine\u2019s is usually more useful for burns covering a large area, while the Palmar Method is more useful in establishing smaller burns.",
  "triage-transport/rapid-fetal-assessment": "23 Rapid Fetal Assessment Notification and Alert Criteria: All obstetrical patients that are greater than 20 weeks gestation and meet the criteria for a Code Yellow Rapid Fetal Assessment, Code Yellow Trauma Alert, Code Red, or Code Blue will be transported to Wesley Medical Center. Rapid Fetal Assessment (RFA) Rapid fetal assessment alerts should be communicated to Dispatch as soon as possible and should include all of the following information: \u2022 Triage \u2022 Age \u2022 GCS \u2022 Mechanism of injury \u2022 Estimated gestational age \u2022 Presence of multiples \u2022 Gravida and Para \u2022 Destination \u2022 ETA Code Yellow Rapid Fetal Assessment Criteria: Any OB patient with one of the following: \u2022 OB patient greater than 20 weeks gestation. \u2022 Blunt trauma with complaints of abdominal and /or back pain. \u2022 Motor vehicle accidents where patient was unrestrained or has seatbelt abrasions to the iliac crests.",
  "triage-transport/stemi-alert": "24 STEMI Alert Criteria: STEMI: ST-Elevation Myocardial Infarctions will be determined using a 12-lead (15-lead/posterior or right-sided 12-lead if necessary). It is defined as ST Elevation present at the J-point in at least two contiguous leads and >5mm. Presence of reciprocal changes helps confirm the diagnosis but is not required for STEMI activation. STEMI patients are to be transported to Via Christi St. Francis, Wesley Medical Center, or Kansas Medical Center. STEMI Alert Notification: All STEMI alerts need to be communicated with dispatch as soon as possible and include the following: \u2022 Triage \u2022 Age \u2022 Gender \u2022 GCS \u2022 Patient\u2019s cardiologist (or specify if patient does not have a cardiologist) \u2022 Destination \u2022 ETA",
  "triage-transport/stroke-alert": "25 Stroke Alert Notification and Criteria: Stroke alerts should be communicated via dispatch as soon as possible and should include all of the following information: \u2022 Triage \u2022 Age \u2022 Gender \u2022 GCS \u2022 Onset of symptoms/last seen normal \u2022 Findings of Cincinnati Stroke Assessment \u2022 Destination \u2022 ETA Stroke Scale Assessment: The Cincinnati Pre-hospital Stroke Scale is a system used to diagnose the presence of a stroke in a patient. It tests three signs for abnormal findings which may indicate that the patient is having a stroke. If any one of the three tests shows abnormal findings, the patient may be having a stroke and should be transported to a hospital as soon as possible. \u2022 Facial droop: Have the person smile or show his/her teeth. If one side doesn't move as well as the other and it seems to droop, that could be sign of a stroke. o Normal: Both sides of face move equally. o Abnormal: One side of face does not move as well as the other (or not at all). \u2022 Arm drift: Have the person close his/her eyes and hold his/her arms straight out in front for about 10 seconds. If one arm does not move, or one arm winds up drifting down more than the other, that could be a sign of a stroke. \u2022 Normal: Both arms move equally or not at all. o Abnormal: One arm does not move or one arm drifts down compared with the other side. \u2022 Speech: Have the person say, \"You can't teach an old dog new tricks,\" or some other simple, familiar saying. If the person slurs the words, gets some words wrong, or is unable to speak, that could be a sign of stroke. o Normal: Patient uses correct words with no slurring. o Abnormal: Slurred or inappropriate words or mute. NOTE: If a family member or the witnessing bystander does not accompany EMS to the hospital, get that person\u2019s phone number for the hospital to contact him/her with any further questions about the patient.",
  "triage-transport/trauma-alert-criteria": "26 Trauma Alert Notification and Criteria: Trauma Alert Notification Trauma alerts should be communicated with Dispatch as soon as possible and should include all of the following information: \u2022 Triage \u2022 Age \u2022 Gender \u2022 GCS. \u2022 Mechanism of injury. \u2022 Major body system injured. \u2022 Destination \u2022 ETA Trauma Alert Criteria \u2022 Code Red Trauma: o GCS of less than 14. o Open or depressed skull fracture. o Systolic blood pressure < 90 mmHg. o Near respiratory and/or cardiac arrest. (Including hangings and traumatic drowning). o Respiratory distress: Adult rates of < 10 or > 29 breaths per minute (<20 in infant age < 1 year). o Penetrating injuries to chest, abdomen, head, neck, and extremities proximal to elbow and knee. o Total or partial amputation, crushed, mangled, or de-gloved extremity proximal to the wrist or ankle. o Two or more open or closed fractures to femur or humerus. o Pelvic fracture with unstable vital signs. o Paralysis. o Flail chest with paradoxical respirations. o Shock present. \u2022 Code Yellow Trauma: o Loss of consciousness greater than 5 minutes. o Pelvic fractures with stable vital signs. o Total or partial amputations distal to the wrist or ankle. o Open or closed fractures with neurovascular compromise. o Falls: \u27a2 Adults \u2013 greater than 20 feet \u27a2 Pediatric under five (5) foot - use two times their height \u27a2 Pediatric over five (5) foot - use 10 feet. o Motor vehicle accidents: \u27a2 Auto vs. pedestrian, motorcycle, or bicyclist thrown, run over, or an impact greater than 20 mph. \u27a2 Motorcycle crash greater than 20 mph. \u27a2 Total or partial ejection from automobile. \u27a2 Death of another occupant in same vehicle as patient. \u27a2 Inside compartment intrusion greater than 12 inches on occupant side and 18 inches on any side. \u27a2 Accidents greater than 35 mph with high index of suspicion for injury. o Co-morbidities and special considerations: \u27a2 Age greater than 55 years old. \u27a2 Bleeding disorders or patient on anticoagulants. \u27a2 Renal disease requiring dialysis.",
  "triage-transport/triage-mass-casualty": "27 Triage Mass Casualty Incident: Any situation that potentially overwhelms the local EMS resources should be declared a Multiple/Mass Casualty Incident. The first responding EMS unit should declare a possible major incident while responding to the scene when the dispatch information suggests the likelihood that one exists. As soon as possible upon arrival at the scene, the unit should verify that a major incident does or does not exist. At a Multiple/Mass Casualty Incident involving mutual aid from several communities, a common EMS frequency should be established for ambulance communications. Patients will be triaged according to START and JumpSTART triage criteria. During primary triage, providers should spend no more than 30 seconds with each patient. Patients may be treated on scene only after all patients have been triaged and staged. ALS providers should consider providing care at the BLS level in order to give care to as many patients as possible. START Triage: \u2022 Should be used for all adult patients. \u2022 Walking wounded should be encouraged to congregate in a designated location under their own strength and triaged in the GREEN (minor) category. \u2022 Patients with no respiratory effort should be triaged in the BLACK (deceased) category following an attempt to open the airway. \u2022 Patients with difficulty in respirations, perfusion or mental status as specified below should be triaged in the RED (immediate) category. \u27a2 Respirations >30/minute \u27a2 Perfusion \u2013 No radial pulse or capillary refill times >2 seconds \u27a2 Mental Status \u2013 Unable to follow simple commands All patients who cannot walk, have respiratory effort, and do not meet criteria for the RED category should be triaged to the YELLOW (delayed) category. 28 29 JumpSTART Triage: \u2022 Should be used for pediatric patients under 14 years of age. \u2022 Walking wounded should be encouraged to congregate in a designated location under their own power and triaged in the GREEN (minor) category. \u2022 Patients with a peripheral pulse but without respiratory effort should receive 5 ventilations then categorized as RED (immediate) if respiratory effort resumes or BLACK (deceased) if apnea continues. \u2022 Position the airway for patients with no respiratory effort or peripheral pulse. No palpable pulse found the patient should be triaged in the BLACK (deceased) category. \u2022 Patients with difficulty in respirations, perfusion or mental status as specified below should be triaged in the RED (immediate) category. \u27a2 Respirations >45/min or <15/min \u27a2 Perfusion \u2013 No peripheral pulse or capillary refill times >2 seconds \u27a2 Mental Status \u2013 unresponsive or responsive to painful stimulus \u2022 All patients who cannot walk, have respiratory effort, and do not meet criteria for the RED category should be triaged to the (delayed) category. 30",
  "triage-transport/glasgow-coma-scale": "31 Glasgow Coma Scale: The Glasgow Coma Scale provides a score in the range 3-15; patients with scores of 3-8 are usually said to be in a coma. The total score is the sum of the scores in three categories. For adults the scores are as follows: Eye Opening Response Spontaneous - open with blinking at baseline. 4 points Opens to verbal command, speech, or shout. 3 points Opens to pain, not applied to face. 2 points None 1 point Verbal Response Oriented 5 points Confused conversation, but able to answer questions. 4 points Inappropriate responses, words discernible. 3 points Incomprehensible speech 2 points None 1 point Motor Response Obeys commands for movement. 6 points Purposeful movement to painful stimulus. 5 points Withdraws from pain. 4 points Abnormal (spastic) flexion, decorticate posture. 3 points Extensor (rigid) response, decerebrate posture. 2 points None 1 point For children under 5, the verbal response criteria are adjusted as follows: SCORE 2 to 5 Years 0 TO 23 Months 5 Appropriate words or phrases Smiles or coos appropriately 4 Inappropriate words Cries and consolable 3 Persistent cries and/or screams Persistent inappropriate crying &/or screaming 2 Grunts Grunts or is agitated or restless 1 No response No response",
  "triage-transport/firearms-air-ambulance-transport": "32 Firearms Security: If during a response, EMS personnel find a firearm, either on the patient or with anyone who might accompany the patient, law enforcement officers, either on scene or in route shall be notified so they can take possession of, and be responsible for its disposition. In the event that EMS needs to leave the scene prior to law enforcement arrival, the firearm shall be secured in a locking storage space in the ambulance with the gun lock secured and the muzzle pointed to the outside of the compartment. In the case of a rifle or shotgun or any firearm being too large for the locking storage space it should be placed in the rearmost compartment on the passenger side in a vertical position with the butt down and barrel pointed upward and secured in place assuring that it will not fall against the door or other equipment in the compartment. \u2756 Note: If the firearm is in its holster, leave it in the holster. \u2756 Note: The firearm can be left at the patient\u2019s residence. On arrival at the emergency facility, notify appropriate law enforcement officials to take possession of the firearm, noting the officer\u2019s name, badge number, and whether law enforcement took possession of the firearm on scene or at our destination, or if the firearm was left at the residence. Air-Ambulance Response: Criteria \u2022 Patient\u2019s condition requires expeditious transport to a hospital capable of providing definitive care. \u2022 The scene is in a remote location with an extended ground transport time. \u2022 The patient\u2019s condition is a \u201clife or limb\u201d threatening situation demanding intensive multi-disciplinary treatment and care. \u2022 Unstable trauma patients as defined by the vital signs and physical findings. (Code Red) \u2022 Critical burn patients. (Code Red) \u2022 Mass Casualty incidents. \u2022 Sufficient area for helicopter landing (100\u2019 X 100\u2019 area) free of trees, power lines, and debris with approach avenues from both the north and south. \u2022 Good clinical judgment should be used at all times during decision-making process. Hazmat Response: Always ensure crew safety during hazmat responses. EMS crews will standby at a safe location upwind and uphill of the scene. Once fire crews have established a safe, \u201cgreen zone,\u201d the EMS crew may enter the area. Crews will take precautionary measures when making patient contact and ensure that the patient has been properly decontaminated prior to transport. Once patient transport has been completed the EMS crew will decontaminate the unit before returning to service. Extrication: During vehicle extrication the EMS crew will wear the provided protective clothing inside the work area established by the fire department.",
  "triage-transport/hazmat-response-extrication": "32 Firearms Security: If during a response, EMS personnel find a firearm, either on the patient or with anyone who might accompany the patient, law enforcement officers, either on scene or in route shall be notified so they can take possession of, and be responsible for its disposition. In the event that EMS needs to leave the scene prior to law enforcement arrival, the firearm shall be secured in a locking storage space in the ambulance with the gun lock secured and the muzzle pointed to the outside of the compartment. In the case of a rifle or shotgun or any firearm being too large for the locking storage space it should be placed in the rearmost compartment on the passenger side in a vertical position with the butt down and barrel pointed upward and secured in place assuring that it will not fall against the door or other equipment in the compartment. \u2756 Note: If the firearm is in its holster, leave it in the holster. \u2756 Note: The firearm can be left at the patient\u2019s residence. On arrival at the emergency facility, notify appropriate law enforcement officials to take possession of the firearm, noting the officer\u2019s name, badge number, and whether law enforcement took possession of the firearm on scene or at our destination, or if the firearm was left at the residence. Air-Ambulance Response: Criteria \u2022 Patient\u2019s condition requires expeditious transport to a hospital capable of providing definitive care. \u2022 The scene is in a remote location with an extended ground transport time. \u2022 The patient\u2019s condition is a \u201clife or limb\u201d threatening situation demanding intensive multi-disciplinary treatment and care. \u2022 Unstable trauma patients as defined by the vital signs and physical findings. (Code Red) \u2022 Critical burn patients. (Code Red) \u2022 Mass Casualty incidents. \u2022 Sufficient area for helicopter landing (100\u2019 X 100\u2019 area) free of trees, power lines, and debris with approach avenues from both the north and south. \u2022 Good clinical judgment should be used at all times during decision-making process. Hazmat Response: Always ensure crew safety during hazmat responses. EMS crews will standby at a safe location upwind and uphill of the scene. Once fire crews have established a safe, \u201cgreen zone,\u201d the EMS crew may enter the area. Crews will take precautionary measures when making patient contact and ensure that the patient has been properly decontaminated prior to transport. Once patient transport has been completed the EMS crew will decontaminate the unit before returning to service. Extrication: During vehicle extrication the EMS crew will wear the provided protective clothing inside the work area established by the fire department.",
  "procedures/medical-procedures-criteria": "34 Medical Procedures Criteria: OBJECTIVE To ensure that considerations of medical procedures to be attempted are based upon or within: \u2022 DO NO HARM o Training given to a Paramedic, AEMT, EMT, and EMR. o Established textbook guidelines. o Boundaries established by the Medical Director of Mulvane EMS. o Recommendations of the Emergency Room Physicians. o Maintaining a consistently high level of care. o Duty to Act. o Standard of Care. \u2022 PROHIBITED ACTIVITIES: The following medical procedures will not be attempted by technicians in the field: o Pericardiocentesis. o Intracardiac medication injections. o Arterial blood samples. o Central venous catheterization of sub-clavian, internal jugular, or femoral. (External jugular catheterization may be attempted without specific physician\u2019s order.) o Gastric lavage.",
  "procedures/adult-cardiac-arrest-ccr": "35 Adult Cardiac Arrest: Cardiocerebral Resuscitation (CCR) Indications: Adult cardiac arrest of suspected cardiac etiology. Treat reversible causes concurrently per appropriate protocol CCR Quality \u2022 Push hard (>2 inches), push fast (110/minute), allow complete recoil. \u25aa NOTE: Defibtech operates at 101/minute; Manual compression rate is to remain at 110/minute \u2022 Minimize interruptions in chest compressions; interruptions should be infrequent and for no longer than 10 seconds. \u2022 Avoid excessive ventilation. Ventilate at a rate of 6 breaths per minute or once every 20th compression. \u2022 Rotate compressor every 220 or when compression quality decreases as indicated by Real CPR or capnography trending. Electrical Therapy \u2022 Biphasic defibrillation: 200 joules. \u2022 AED settings are preset by manufacturer. Medication Therapy Normal Saline IV/IO TKO or bolus (if indicated) up to 2000 ml. Epinephrine 1:10,000 1 mg IV/IO repeat every 5 minutes up to 5 mg; No more than 5 Epi total regardless of rhythm changes Pulseless VF/VT antidysrhythmic options: o Amiodarone 300 mg in 20mL NS IV/IO; Repeat once in 3-5 minutes at 150 mg in 20mL NS IV/IO OR o Lidocaine 1.0-1.5 mg/kg IV/IO; Repeat every 3-5 minutes to maximum of 3 mg/kg. o Magnesium Sulfate 2 gm IV/IO bolus diluted in normal saline (for Torsades De Pointes only). Consider Sodium BiCarb 1mEq/kg after 10 minutes; Repeat once in 5 minutes at 0.5 mEq/kg (Asystole, PEA, VF, VT). DO NOT ADMINISTER AMIODARONE AND SODIUM BICARBONATE IN THE SAME LINE Begin chest compressions & apply metronome Suction/clear airway PRN OPA Oxygen via NRB @ 10-15 LPM Utilize Zoll Real CPR Drug Therapy: IV/IO access Epinephrine every 5 minutes Amiodarone for refractory VF/VT Lidocaine every 3-5 minutes VF/VT Magnesium for Torsades De Pointes After 6 minutes (660 compressions): Ventilations q 20th compression With BVM w/O2 @ 15 LPM Consider Advanced Airway Monitor ETCO2 with advanced airway",
  "procedures/adult-cardiac-arrest-cpr": "36 Adult Cardiac Arrest: Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR) Indications: Adult cardiac arrest of suspected non-cardiac etiology. Treat reversible causes concurrently per appropriate protocol CPR Quality \u2022 Push hard (>2 inches), push fast (110/minute), allow complete recoil. o NOTE: Defibtech operates at 101/minute; Manual compression rate is to remain at 110/minute \u2022 Minimize interruptions in chest compressions; interruptions should be infrequent and for no longer than 10 seconds. \u2022 Avoid excessive ventilation. Ventilate at a rate of 6 breaths per minute or once every 20th compression. \u2022 One-person Adult CPR: 30:2 compression-ventilation ratio. Initiate continuous compressions with adequate resources. Electrical Therapy \u2022 Biphasic defibrillation: 200 joules. \u2022 AED settings are preset by manufacturer. Medication Therapy Normal Saline IV/IO TKO or bolus if indicated up to 2000 ml. Epinephrine 1:10,000 1 mg IV/IO, repeat every 5 minutes up to 5 mg; No more than 5 Epi total regardless of rhythm changes Pulseless VF/VT antidysrhythmic options: o Amiodarone 300 mg in 20mL NS IV/IO; Repeat once in 3-5 minutes at 150 mg in 20mL NS IV/IO OR o Lidocaine 1.0-1.5 mg/kg IV/IO; Repeat every 3-5 minutes to maximum of 3 mg/kg. o Magnesium Sulfate 2 gm IV/IO bolus diluted in normal saline (for Torsades De Pointes only). Consider Sodium BiCarb 1mEq/kg after 10 minutes; Repeat once in 5 minutes at 0.5 mEq/kg (Asystole, PEA, VF, VT). DO NOT ADMINISTER AMIODARONE AND SODIUM BICARBONATE IN THE SAME LINE Start CPR & apply metronome Suction/clear airway PRN OPA BVM Ventilations q 20th compression Utilize Zoll Real CPR Drug Therapy: IV/IO access Epinephrine every 5 minutes Amiodarone for refractory VF/VT Lidocaine for refractory VF/VT Magnesium for Torsades De Pointes Consider Advanced Airway Monitor ETCO2 with advanced airway",
  "procedures/aed": "37 Automatic External Defibrillator: The Automatic External Defibrillator (AED) may only be used on an unconscious, pulseless, and apneic patient. Indication \u2022 The patient must weigh 90 pounds and/or have a large enough physical stature that the patches have ample space to be properly placed on the body. \u2022 Insure that the patient is unconscious, pulseless, and apneic. \u2022 Do not apply to patients that have a valid DNR, DPO, or Living Will that indicate that the patient does not want to be resuscitated. \u2022 Follow the manufacturer\u2019s recommendations for adult and pediatric placement of patches and size of patches. Precautions \u2022 Do not use the AED in explosive or hazardous environments. \u2022 Do not touch the patient or electrodes while the AED is analyzing or defibrillating. \u2022 Do not place the patches directly over an implanted defibrillator or pacemaker. \u2022 The patient must have a patent airway or the use of the AED will not be productive. Procedure \u2022 Determine that the patient is unconscious, pulseless, and apneic. \u2022 If the arrest is unwitnessed CPR must be started for no less than two minutes. \u2022 Apply the appropriately sized patches to the patient in the sterna and apex positions (shown on the patches). \u2022 Turn on the AED and ANALYZE without touching the patient, AED, or the cables. \u2022 If the AED advises \u201cSHOCK INDICATED\u201d, at that time the patient must be cleared of all providers and only then can the SHOCK button be pressed. \u2022 Resume CPR and ventilations with a BVM for two minutes and then the pulse should be checked. \u2022 If the AED advises \u201cNO SHOCK INDICATED\u201d, CPR must be immediately started. \u2022 Resume CPR and ventilations with a BVM for two minutes and then the pulse should be checked. Documentation \u2022 The application, number of shocks, and the results of the patient must be properly recorded and documented in the patient care report.",
  "procedures/chest-decompression": "38 Chest Decompression/Needle Thoracentesis: A life-threatening situation may arise when a \u201cone-way\u201d valve is created that allows air to enter but not leave the pleural space. As the pressure in the pleural space exceeds the outside atmospheric pressure, the physiologic consequences of a simple pneumothorax are magnified to include tension pneumothorax. Increasing pressure within the pleural space further compromises pulmonary function/capacity and will force the mediastinum to the contra lateral side. This will result in increasing difficulty breathing (respirations) and a decrease in blood flow to the heart. As the pleural space increases, the mediastinum shifts and will result in a high venous pressure caused by the kinking of the Vena Cava. This causes a reduced blood flow into the heart and decreased cardiac output. Signs and Symptoms \u2022 Extreme anxiety, cyanosis, tachypnea, diminished or absent breath sounds to the injured hemisphere, bulging of the intercostal muscles, hyper-resonance, jugular vein distention, tachycardia, narrowing pulse pressures, hypotension, subcutaneous emphysema, and tracheal deviation (if seen - a late sign). \u2022 Cyanosis and decreased breath sounds are the most common early findings with a pneumothorax. Other signs and symptoms develop later as pressure increases. Tachycardia and increased dyspnea despite treatment may be obvious later. JVD and tracheal deviation are among the latest of the signs and symptoms. Authorized Use \u2022 For relief of tension pneumothorax by standing order. \u2022 For relief of pneumothorax by physician order. Insertion site \u2022 Second intercostal space \u2022 Mid-clavicular line or axillary line if MCL site does not relieve pneumothorax, unable to access MCL, or if patient is obese. \u2022 Same side as injury Contraindications \u2022 None Technique \u2022 Cleanse the site with betadine. \u2022 Twist off the red cap and remove the 14 ga needle and catheter \u2022 Insert at a 90-degree angle to the chest wall into the intercostal space over the superior border of the third rib, midclavicular line. If using anterior axillary site, insert at 90 degree angle to the chest wall into the 5th intercostal space over the superior border of the 6th rib, approximately a hands width from the axilla. \u2022 Insert into the pleural space while listening for the sudden escape of air. \u2022 Remove the needle portion and leave the catheter in place. \u2022 Secure to the chest with a chest seal securing it adhesive side down with the valve directly over the catheter. \u2022 Press dressing firmly in place to assure an occlusive seal. \u2022 Re-evaluate breath sounds and vital signs. \u2022 Monitor closely for recurrence of respiratory distress. PEDIATRIC TECHNIQUE \u2022 Insertion site is 2nd ICS mid-clavicular line ONLY \u2022 Ages 0-1 years: use 22g IV needle \u2022 Ages 1-4 years: use 20g IV needle",
  "procedures/cpap": "39 Continuous Positive Airway Pressure (CPAP): Continuous Positive Airway Pressure has been shown to rapidly improve vital signs, gas exchange, reduce the work of breathing, decrease the sense of dyspnea, and decrease the need for endotracheal intubation in patients who suffer from shortness of breath from asthma, COPD, pulmonary edema, CHF, and pneumonia. In patients with CHF, CPAP improves hemodynamics by reducing left ventricular preload and afterload. Indications Any patient who is in respiratory distress with signs and symptoms consistent with asthma, COPD, pulmonary edema, CHF, or pneumonia and who is: \u2022 Awake and able to follow commands. \u2022 Is over 8 years old and is able to fit the CPAP mask. \u2022 Has the ability to maintain an open airway. \u2022 And exhibits two or more of the following: o A respiratory rate greater than 25 breaths per minute. o SPO2 of less than 94% at any time. o Use of accessory muscles during respirations. Contraindications \u2022 Patient is in respiratory arrest/apneic. \u2022 Patient is suspected of having a pneumothorax or has suffered chest trauma. \u2022 Patient has a tracheostomy. \u2022 Patient is actively vomiting or has upper GI bleeding. Procedure \u2022 Explain the procedure to the patient. \u2022 Ensure adequate oxygen supply to ventilation device. \u2022 Place the patient on continuous pulse oximetry. \u2022 Place the patient on cardiac monitor and record rhythm strip. \u2022 Place the patient on EtCO2 monitoring via the nasal cannula before placing the mask but DO NOT break the seal to apply the cannula. \u2022 Place the delivery device over the mouth and nose. \u2022 Secure the mask with provided straps or other provided devices. \u2022 For CHF/Pulmonary Edema, titrate to 10 cm/H2O. For all other asthma & COPD patients titrate to 2-5 cm H2O. \u2022 Check for air leaks. \u2022 Monitor and document the patient\u2019s respiratory response to treatment. \u2022 Check and document vital signs every 5 minutes. \u2022 Continue to coach patient to keep mask in place and readjust as needed. \u2022 Contact medical control to advise them of CPAP initiation. \u2022 If respiratory status deteriorates, remove device and consider intermittent positive pressure ventilation via BVM, and/or placement of non-visualized airway, or endotracheal intubation. Removal Procedure \u2022 CPAP therapy needs to be continuous and should not be removed unless the patient cannot tolerate the mask, experiences respiratory arrest, or begins to vomit. \u2022 Intermittent positive pressure ventilation with a BVM, placement of a non-visualized airway, and/or endotracheal intubation should be considered if the patient is removed from CPAP therapy. Special Notes \u2022 Do not remove CPAP until hospital therapy is ready to be placed on patient. \u2022 Watch patient for gastric distention which can result in vomiting. \u2022 Due to changes in preload and afterload of the heart during CPAP therapy, a complete set of vital signs must be obtained every 5 minutes. \u2022 Consider 0.5-1.0 mg Lorazepam IV/IO/IM/IN OR 2mg Midazolam IV/IO/IM/IN for severe anxiety to aid the patient in accepting the mask.",
  "procedures/defibtech": "40 Defibtech Procedure: Indication: For use during cardiac arrest to provide chest compressions NOTE: The Defibtech compression rate is set at 101 compressions/min. However, manual compression rate is to remain at 110 compressions/min. Contraindications: \u2022 Defibtech cannot be placed safely or correctly on patient\u2019s chest \u2022 Patient is too small for starting piston height to reach the patient\u2019s chest \u2022 Patient is too large for the frame to attach to the backboard OR if the piston cannot be mounted without compressing the patient\u2019s chest Procedure: \u2022 Place soft cot under patient while also positioning the Defibtech backboard (grey curved piece) under the patient, lining the top of it up with patient\u2019s axillary creases. \u2022 Roll patient onto soft cot and Defibtech backboard, re-positioning the backboard as needed for proper alignment. The center of the backboard should be in line with patient\u2019s nipple line. \u2022 Attach compression module to frame, ensuring battery is placed properly in the compression module. \u2022 Press ON/OFF button for one second to turn ON. \u2022 Adjust piston height, ensuring proper positioning on patient\u2019s chest. \u2022 Press Run button to perform compressions. \u2022 PAUSE button is to be pressed prior to rhythm checks and pressed again to resume compressions. \u2022 To ensure Defibtech remains appropriately positioned, apply the Stabilization Strap behind patient\u2019s neck and connect Strap to the frame, adjusting length as needed. \u2022 Hands may be secured to the frame using the provided straps, however ensure IV access is not impeded.",
  "procedures/external-cardiac-pacing": "41 External Cardiac Pacing: The use of the External Transcutaneous Pacemaker is a temporary pacing device that may be used for rapid deployment in life threatening situations. The external pacemaker is to be used on symptomatic patients only or when all other means of treatment have been exhausted. The provider must provide basic life support measures first. Also, externally pacing a patient is most productive when in combination with drug therapy. Pediatric patients should have the appropriately sized patches applied. Indications \u2022 Symptomatic Bradycardia Considerations \u2022 Avoid hazardous environments, such as, flammable atmospheres, wet or moist environments, and conductive areas. \u2022 Pacing a patient may be painful or discomforting. Consider pain management prior to pacing a patient if they have a low pain tolerance. Application \u2022 The application should follow the manufacturer\u2019s recommendations. \u2022 Use appropriate size patches for the patient. The patient should have ample space between patches that they don\u2019t touch. \u2022 The rate should be no less than 60 bpm for adults and children and 100 bpm for infants. Procedure \u2022 Ensure 4-lead is applied properly. \u2022 Attach pacing pads, ensuring good contact with skin (i.e. remove clothing, hair, and moisture). Ensure pads are not covering any part of other electrodes. \u2022 Select the lead (I, II, or III) providing the largest amplitude QRS complex. \u2022 Verify R-waves are properly being detected. \u2022 Press the PACER button. It will default to DEMAND mode. \u2022 Set Pacer Rate. \u2022 Turn On Pacer. \u2022 Set Pacer Output. \u2022 Determine presence of capture: o Electrical capture = presence of widened QRS complex, loss of any underlying intrinsic rhythm, and appearance of an extended, and sometimes enlarged, T-wave o Mechanical capture = presence of palpable peripheral pulse matching the pacer; USE ONLY femoral artery, right brachial artery, or right radial artery to check for capture Contraindications \u2022 Do not pace the following patients: o Trauma patients, except in electrocutions. o Hypothermic patients. o Patients on which the patches do not properly fit.",
  "procedures/fire-rehab": "42 Fire Rehab: Fire rehab is established so that personnel can seek rehabilitation from an incident and/or medical assistance when needed. Indication \u2022 Fire rehab will be established when requested by command for the following incidents: o Working fires. o Extended scene times on motor-vehicle accidents. o Large grass fires. o Full response Haz-Mat. o Any collapse or trench rescues with extended scene times o Extended scene times in inclement weather. o At the discretion of the Incident Commander. Assignment \u2022 All firefighters/personnel will report to rehab as a unit. \u2022 One crew member will become Rehab Officer and notify command. \u2022 All firefighters/personnel will remain in rehab for no less than 20 minutes. \u2022 The unit assigned to rehab will not be released until all firefighters/personnel have been cleared. \u2022 When a unit is assigned to rehab, the time is for them to relax. They will not be permitted to conduct duties on the incident grounds until released. Assessment \u2022 Anytime a formal rehab is established all firefighters/personnel assigned to rehab will need an initial set and exiting set of vitals. o Vitals will include a BP, Temp, HR, and LOC. \u27a2 BP must be less than 160 mmHg systolic. \u27a2 TEMP below 101\u00b0. \u27a2 HR must be below 110bpm. \u27a2 SpO\u00b2 that sustains at >92%. \u27a2 CO that sustains at 9% or lower. o All vitals must be below recommended limits prior to release from rehab. o The provider may use their discretion on limits due to patients PMHX. o At any time that an individual in rehab becomes symptomatic to medical problems or traumatic injuries that individual will become a patient and be treated as such. Treatment \u2022 Every unit in rehab should be able to relax in a relatively controlled environment. \u2022 Water and/or Gatorade will be available to drink for rehydration. \u2022 Cooling towels and/or blankets should be available.",
  "procedures/intramuscular-medication-administration": "43 Intramuscular Medication (IM) Administration: Drugs administered by the intramuscular (IM) route are deposited into vascular muscle tissue, which allows for rapid absorption into the circulation. Indications: When medication administration is necessary, and the medication must be given via IM route or as an alternative route in selected medications. Contraindications \u2022 Active infection, cellulitis, or dermatitis at the site of administration \u2022 Known allergy or hypersensitivity to the drug \u2022 Acute myocardial infarction- the release of muscle enzymes may provide a confounding bias in making the diagnosis \u2022 Thrombocytopenia \u2022 Coagulation defects \u2022 Hypovolemic shock- the absorption of the drug may be hampered owing to compromised vascularity to the muscle Procedure \u2022 Explain the procedure and gain consent. \u2022 Before drug administration, check whether the patient has any allergies. \u2022 Check the prescription is correct, following the \u2018five rights\u2019 of drug administration to reduce the risk of error. \u2022 Assemble the syringe and needle and withdraw the required amount of drug from the ampoule. Some medicines are available in pre-filled syringes and manufacturer\u2019s instructions should be followed. \u2022 Disperse air bubbles from the syringe. \u2022 Expose the selected area and cleanse the injection site with alcohol. \u2022 Insert the needle into the skin with a smooth, steady motion \u2022 90-degree angle skin pinched skin flattened \u2022 Aspirate for blood \u2022 Inject the medication. \u2022 Withdraw the needle quickly and dispose of properly without recapping. \u2022 Apply pressure to the site. \u2022 Apply band aid \u2022 Monitor the patient for the desired therapeutic effects as well as any possible side effects. \u2022 Document the medication, dose, route, and time on/with the patient care report (PCR).",
  "procedures/intranasal-medication-administration": "44 Intranasal Medication (IN) Administration: IN medication administration is not intended to be a substitute for establishing IV access but is instead intended to be utilized in the emergent situation when IV access is not readily or quickly available. Contraindications \u2022 Severe nasal trauma \u2022 Epistaxis \u2022 Known prior nasal cocaine usage (relative) Procedure \u2022 Draw up medication in syringe. \u2022 Attach MAD device to syringe. \u2022 Place administration end of device gently against the patient\u2019s nare to form a good quality seal. \u2022 Administer no more than 1 mL of solution into each nare per delivery. o Do not administer medication during patient exhalation through nose.",
  "procedures/intraosseous-access-infusion": "45 Intraosseous Access/Infusion: The EZ IO device consists of a battery powered driver, a special needle assembly, a right-angle extension tubing (\u201cEZ Connect\u201d), and a wrist band. Indications \u2022 Patients where rapid, regular IV access is unavailable after 90 seconds with any of the following; o Cardiac arrest / Respiratory Failure / Respiratory Arrest o Multi-system Trauma with severe hypovolemia. o Severe dehydration with vascular collapse and/or loss of consciousness. o Severe Sepsis Contraindications \u2022 Fracture proximal to proposed insertion site \u2022 History of Osteogenesis Imperfecta (\u201cbrittle bone disease\u201d) \u2022 Current or prior infection at the proposed site \u2022 Previous IO insertion or joint replacement at the proposed site \u2022 Traumatized extremity \u2022 Cellulitis \u2022 Burns that may be infected by the technique used for insertion \u2022 Congenital bone disease Potential Complications o Technical: Improper placement may result in sub-periosteal or soft tissue infusion; Penetration of posterior wall of medullary cavity resulting in soft tissue infusion and subsequent compartment syndrome; Slow infusion from clotting of marrow. o Systemic: Osteomyelitis; Fat embolism; Slight periostitis at the injection site (usually short term); Bone fracture Sites \u2022 Proximal Tibia: Two finger widths below the patella or tibial tuberosity. Find the \u201cflat spot\u201d on the medial aspect of the tibial shaft. Remember: \u201cBig Toe IO\u201d means to look on the big toe side of the leg for the tibial plateau (the flat spot). \u2022 Distal Tibia: Two finger widths above the ankle bone on the medial side. Again: \u201cBig Toe IO\u201d. \u2022 Humeral Head: The greater tubercle is located by having the patient place his hand on his belly button and relax his shoulder and elbow against the stretcher or turn the patient\u2019s arm with the elbow facing out and palm facing in. Slide your thumb up the anterior shaft of the humerus until you feel the greater tubercle; this is the surgical neck. Approximately 1 cm above the surgical neck is the insertion site for most adult patients. Vidacare recommends the 45 mm needle on patients >40kg (90lbs). 46 Insertion: \u2022 Cleanse the site with betadine using aseptic cleansing techniques. \u2022 Penetrate the skin to the bone at a 90-degree angle without running the driver. o Humeral site: point needle at 45 degrees to the horizontal plane, resulting in the needle hub lying perpendicular to the skin. \u2022 Assure the 5mm mark is visible when the needle tip has come in contact with the bone. This assures there will be enough needle length to reach the marrow cavity. If the 5mm mark is not visible do not proceed, the needle is not long enough! \u2022 Run the driver constantly with mild pressure for adult patients, no pressure with pediatric patients. With pediatrics, let the weight of the driver do the work. Stop the needle once it has become flush with the skin or the needle has entered the marrow. \u2022 While holding the hub in place against the skin, unscrew the needle and remove stylet and attach supplied right angle lock. \u2022 Aspirate a small amount of blood and marrow to confirm placement. \u2022 Prior to flushing with normal saline administer 40 mg of 2% Lidocaine for conscious patients or 0.5mg/kg for pediatrics not to exceed 40 mg; flush should be easy. May repeat 20 mg X 1 for comfort of the conscious patient. Wait 60 seconds before flushing with normal saline. \u2022 A second flush may be needed on larger patients; do the second flush with normal saline. \u2022 Check for any leakage or extravasation or fluid gathering in extremity compartments. \u2022 Put the arm band on the patient\u2019s wrist. o Humeral site: secure patients arm in place across the abdomen or to patient\u2019s side ensuring that the elbow remains facing out and the palm remains facing in. \u2022 Attach standard IV set and pressure infuser and infuse fluids as specified. \u2022 Secure needle with tape and/or gauze. \u2022 Document.",
  "procedures/intravenous-access": "47 Intravenous Access: \u2022 IV access may be obtained on any patient deemed necessary by the AEMT or Paramedic for any type of medical or trauma complaint. \u2022 Isolated orthopedic trauma patients may be triaged code green and still receive an IV. \u2022 Code yellow patients or higher require IV access unless the patient refuses the procedure. \u2022 Adult code red patients responsive or unresponsive require a minimum of two attempts before moving on to an IO access. Conscious patients require a physician order prior to starting an IO. \u2022 Pediatric code red patients (responsive or unresponsive) require one attempt prior to starting an IO. Conscious patients require a physician order prior to starting an IO. \u2022 Adult code blue patients without IV access established within 90 seconds should have IO access established ASAP. \u2022 Pediatric code blue patients require one good faith attempt prior to IO access. (A good faith attempt means you looked and were unable to find and obtain IV access within 30 seconds) \u2022 The approved method of obtaining IV access for medical or trauma patients is by using the IV lock and flush system. If the patient requires fluid resuscitation or the patient requires several IV medication administrations, then a drip set and bag of normal saline may be set up with the IV lock system to facilitate these treatments. o Normal Saline bags are to be used for fluid resuscitation or in the event of several medication administrations and medication drips only.",
  "procedures/needle-cricothyrotomy": "48 Needle Cricothyrotomy: The only indication for a needle cricothyrotomy is to secure an airway when ALL OTHER METHODS have been unsuccessful. Needle cricothyrotomies may only be performed by full-time paramedics who have received training within in the last 3 years (either via MEMS or the Difficult Airway Course) AND have approval from the receiving physician through on-line medical control. Once an order for a needle cricothyrotomy has been approved, only then may it be performed. Needle cricothyrotomy is to be performed on patients 11 years and younger. (If 12 years or older, surgical cricothyrotomy should be performed.) EQUIPMENT: \u2022 Iodine or other antiseptic solution \u2022 Gloves and full-face protection \u2022 Appropriate-sized IV catheter (14g, 16g, or 18g) \u2022 3mL syringe w/ 1mL of NS drawn up \u2022 10 drop set cut to approximately 18\u201d, keeping the hub adapter \u2022 2.5 or 3.0 ET tube connector \u2022 BVM/Oxygen/Suction \u2022 Tape PROCEDURE: \u2022 Have suction supplies available and ready. \u2022 Locate the cricothyroid membrane utilizing anatomical landmarks. \u2022 Use non-dominant hand to secure/stabilize membrane. \u2022 Cleanse the area with iodine. \u2022 Draw up 1mL of NS into a 3mL syringe and attach to the IV needle supplied in the cricothyrotomy kit. \u2022 Insert the needle through the cricothyroid membrane at a 45-degree angle toward the feet. \u2022 Aspirate for air with the syringe throughout the procedure of insertion. \u2022 Once air bubbles return easily, stop advancing the needle. \u2022 Advance the catheter into the trachea. \u2022 Attach the modified drop set and secure with tape. \u2022 Provide ventilation and verify placement with capnography, condensation in the tube, and breath sounds.",
  "procedures/restraint-procedures": "49 Restraint Procedures: Procedure Statement To ensure a safe and proper usage of restraint devices for medical and legal purposes. \u2022 Restraints may be used at the discretion of the attending Tech if a patient shows to be a threat to the safety of themselves or others. \u2022 Handcuffs can be used in place of restraints on any patient in the custody of law enforcement that is transported via EMS. The officer must be in the patient compartment during transport. \u2022 Any patient restrained by EMS will be monitored continuously by visual means until another competent health care provider is physically in attendance and assumes medical responsibility for the patient. \u2022 Anytime restraints are used, the need for their use must be documented in the patient narrative. Include in the narrative the time restraints were applied, how the patient was restrained, and any injuries the patient may have sustained while restrained. \u2022 DO NOT USE restraint positions that could impede the patient\u2019s airway (i.e., choke holds, hogtie, prone, etc.) \u2022 Restraints should not be routinely used. Assess each patient carefully, as a unique individual, to determine if restraints are truly necessary to ensure the safety of you and the patient. \u2022 Properly apply and adjust restraints to maintain proper body alignment and patient comfort. Monitor and re- evaluate the restraints at least every 10 minutes. The patient\u2019s condition should be monitored regularly. Make sure the restraints are not cutting off circulation. \u2022 Consider chemical restraint early to prevent overexertion of patient. \u2022 BLUE GOES ON WRISTS \u2022 RED GOES ON ANKLES",
  "procedures/spinal-motion-restriction": "50 Spinal Motion Restriction: Spinal Motion Restriction is defined as the application of a cervical collar and maintaining the spine in neutral alignment. Indication \u2022 Point tenderness on palpation of entire length of spinous processes. \u2022 Focal deficit (motor or sensory) on neuro exam. \u2022 High risk patients: o Vehicle ejection. o Motorcycle crash > 20 mph. o Auto vs. Pedestrian or bicycle at > 20 mph. o Axial load to head. o Fall from 3x patient\u2019s height. \u2022 Low risk patients: o Patient not reliable and competent. \u27a2 Below baseline level of alertness. \u27a2 Evidence of clinical intoxication. \u27a2 Distracting injury. \u27a2 Unable to communicate adequately. Procedure \u2022 Perform appropriate trauma assessment. \u2022 Determine if required by mechanism of injury. \u2022 Criteria for spinal motion restriction met, patient should be transported flat and supine on the cot reinforcing the importance of limiting movement of cervical spine and encourage neutral positioning. o Perform a brief neurologic assessment to assess presence of movement and sensation to light touch in all extremities. o Evaluate: \u27a2 Cervical spine for point tenderness and step-off \u27a2 Neck for other injuries or findings o Place an appropriately sized c-collar. \u2022 Ambulatory or able to self-extricate, assist to supine position on cot while limiting spinal movement. \u2022 Extrication needed: o A long spine-board with a cervical collar in place may be used - only as a tool - and should be removed once extrication is complete and patient is supine on the cot maintaining neutral spine alignment throughout the process. o KED may be used if no other satisfactory method is available. It is not to be used for neuro intact patients seated in vehicles or when rapid extrication is required. If used, it should be unbuckled and fully released (including head straps) once the patient is laid flat on the cot. \u2022 Special Situations: (Monitor and document neuro status in these alternate positions) o Vomiting: It is acceptable to place patient on their side to facilitate airway protection with repeated vomiting. o Dyspnea: If dyspneic with the cervical collar in place while laying completely flat, the head of the cot may be slightly elevated no more than 20 degrees. o Pregnancy (third trimester): To facilitate adequate venous return the patient may be placed on their left side or elevate the head of the cot no more than 20 degrees.",
  "procedures/surgical-cricothyrotomy": "51 Surgical Cricothyrotomy: The only indication for a surgical cricothyrotomy is to secure an airway when ALL OTHER METHODS have been unsuccessful. Surgical cricothyrotomies may only be performed by full-time paramedics who have received training within in the last 3 years (either via MEMS or the Difficult Airway Course) AND have approval from the receiving physician through on-line medical control. Once an order for a surgical cricothyrotomy has been approved, only then may it be performed. Pt must be 12 years of age or older to have surgical cricothyrotomy performed. (If under age 12, needle cricothyrotomy must be performed.) Equipment: \u2022 Iodine or other antiseptic solution \u2022 Scalpel \u2022 Gloves and full-face protection \u2022 10mL syringe for ETT cuff \u2022 ET tube 6mm cuffed \u2022 Bougie \u2022 Sterile 4x4 \u2022 Tape \u2022 BVM/Oxygen \u2022 Suction Procedure: \u2022 Have suction supplies available and ready \u2022 Position patient in the neutral recumbent position. Maintain C-spine precautions for suspected cervical injury. \u2022 Locate the cricothyroid membrane utilizing anatomical landmarks. \u2022 Use non-dominant hand to secure/stabilize membrane. \u2022 Cleanse the area with iodine. \u2022 Make an approximately 2cm incision vertically over the larynx, then re-identify the cricothyroid membrane. Make a horizontal incision through the cricothyroid membrane until the widest part of the blade is inserted through the membrane. \u2022 Place a finger in the hole to maintain patency. \u2022 Place bougie in the hole, using finger as a guide and insert until resistance is felt. You might feel \u201cclicks\u201d along the way from the cricoid rings and the tip of the bougie should be in the carina. \u2022 Slide cuffed ETT 6.0 over bougie and place in trachea. Inflate bulb and secure tube. \u2022 Provide ventilation and verify placement with capnography, condensation in the tube, and bilateral breath sounds. \u2022 Secure tube with tape. \u2022 Make sure patients is reassessed often and tube is secure.",
  "procedures/synchronized-cardioversion": "52 Synchronized Cardioversion: Synchronized cardioversion is the preferred treatment for symptomatic tachycardic dysrhythmias. These patients must be symptomatic and show signs of cardiovascular compromise. Indications & Settings \u2022 Unstable V-Tach: 100 joules, 150 joules, and 200 joules. \u2022 Unstable Supraventricular Tach: 50 joules, 100 joules, 150 joules, and 200 joules. \u2022 Unstable new onset A-Fib: 100 joules, 150 joules, and 200 joules. \u2022 Unstable A-Flutter: 50 joules, 100 joules, 150 joules, and 200 joules. Application \u2022 First provide basic life support measures. \u2022 Obtain orders if indicated by protocols. \u2022 Consider sedation prior to cardioversion if indicated. Procedure \u2022 Select a lead on the monitor that detects an \u201cR\u201d wave. \u2022 Set the monitor to \u201cSync.\u201d Ensure that the \u201cR\u201d wave is getting capture. \u2022 Once capture is indicated, cardio-vert the patient to the specified energy setting stated in the protocols. \u2022 If the rhythm changes follow appropriate protocol for treatment.",
  "procedures/taser-dart-removal": "53 Taser Dart Removal and Care: A taser works by firing two wire-attached darts that can strike a suspect from up to 15 feet or more. It delivers up to 50,000 volts of electricity to the recipient. This jolt of electricity is not harmful to vital body functions such as heart rhythm, pacemaker function or respirations. The taser dart usually penetrates the skin only a few millimeters. Technicians can safely remove a dart simply by pulling it out. The only exception is involvement of the eye, face, neck, breast, groin, spinal column or joint. In this case, leave the dart in place and transport the patient to the hospital for dart removal. Strongly consider scene safety and measures to protect yourself and other rescuers from a potentially violent patient in situations when a taser gun has been used. You do not need to transport a person to the hospital based solely on taser dart exposure. If a patient has no need for further medical evaluation, they can be left in police custody. \u2022 Scene Safety. \u2022 Confirm that the taser has been shut off and the probe is no longer connected to the taser gun. \u2022 Obtain vital signs at the earliest opportunity. Violent and combative behavior may be secondary to intoxication, psychosis, hypoxia, hypoglycemia, OD or CNS infection. Treat trauma and seizure as applicable. \u2022 Evaluate the anatomical location of the probe(s) puncture zone(s). High-risk/sensitive zones will require transport to a medical facility for removal. They Include: o Eyes, ears, nose, mouth and neck. (Darts to scalp, and low risk areas of forehead and cheek, can be removed in the field, but these wounds may require assessment by a physician). o Breasts (female) o Genitalia o Hands or Feet o Joints o Spinal column Removal Procedure \u2022 Utilize PPE. \u2022 Prior to probe removal inform all caregivers that you are about to remove the contaminated sharp. \u2022 Place hand in the form of a \u201cV\u201d around the taser dart in order to stabilize the surrounding skin and to keep loose skin from coming up with the dart. Firmly grasp the probe and with one smooth hard jerk, remove probe from skin. (remove probes one at a time) \u2022 Examine the probe and the patient closely in an effort to make sure the probe tip did not break off during removal. Accordingly, it is important that the person removing the barb visually inspect it to make sure that the tip is fully intact. If the barb remains in the subject, the patient will need to be transported to a medical facility for barb removal. \u2022 Be careful to avoid accidental needle sticks when removing probes. \u2022 Removed probes should be handled like contaminated sharps and should be placed in the specimen container provided by the PD. They will likely log the probes into evidence and 6 inches of the wire must remain intact with the taser buds if the patient is transported. \u2022 Provide wound care by cleansing the affected area with saline and apply a band aid. \u2022 Inform patient of basic wound care and the need to seek additional care in the event that signs of infection occur (redness-fever-drainage-swelling etc.) \u2022 Clear and thorough documentation is required in the body of the report narrative whether or not EMS transports the patient. \u2022 If transport is necessary, transport to the closest appropriate hospital.",
  "procedures/topical-hemostatic-agent": "54 TOPICAL HEMOSTATIC AGENT APPLICATION, ADULT & PEDIATRIC Indications: o Life-threatening hemorrhage to junctional areas of the neck, axilla, and/or groin o Severe scalp lacerations w/ uncontrolled bleeding o Life-threatening hemorrhage to extremities unable to be controlled by direct pressure and/or tourniquet usage Contraindications: o Open thoracic, abdominal, and/or penetrating head wounds Background: QuikClot Combat Gauze is a sterile gauze (rolled or in 4x4 pads) impregnated with kaolin, a compound shown to increase clotting in severe hemorrhage by activating factor XII of the clotting cascade. Kaolin is a naturally occurring inorganic compound that has not been found to cause any allergic reactions. Application: 1. Apply direct pressure to the wound and a tourniquet as applicable. 2. Remove excess pooled blood from around the wound while preserving any clots already in the wound. 3. For junctional and extremity injuries, pack as much rolled QuikClot gauze into the wound as possible and use it to apply pressure directly to the bleeding source until the bleeding stops. More than one package of hemostatic agent may be needed. a. For severe scalp lacerations: apply the 4x4 QuikClot gauze over the wound, DO NOT PACK, and apply direct pressure to the bleeding source until bleeding stops. More than one package may be needed. b. For adjunct therapy for extremity wounds uncontrolled by direct pressure and/or tourniquet, pack as much rolled QuikClot gauze into the wound as possible and use it to apply pressure directly to the bleeding source until the bleeding stops. 4. Continue applying direct pressure to the wound for 3-5 minutes. 5. Leave the hemostatic agent in place. Place empty package over wound to ensure it is transported w/ the patient to the destination facility. 6. Dress the wound with a pressure dressing to secure the hemostatic agent. a. In the case of severe wounds to the neck, direct pressure may have to be continuously applied throughout transport. NOTE: It is extremely important that direct pressure is provided immediately upon application of the hemostatic agent and held for at least 3-5 minutes.",
  "adult/cardiac-arrest": "56 Adult Cardiac Arrest: EMR EMT \u2022 Determine use of CCR versus CPR by suspect etiology. \u2022 Begin CPR/CCR \u2022 Attach AED \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy via BVM if performing CPR OR NRB if performing CCR. EMR and EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Determine and document rhythm in two leads. \u2022 Biphasic defibrillation 200 joules. (VF/VT) Normal Saline IV/IO TKO or bolus if indicated up to 2000 ml. Epinephrine 1mg IV/IO 1:10,000 (repeat 3-5 minutes) up to 5 mg; No more than 5 Epi total regardless of rhythm changes \u2022 Pulseless VF/VT anti-dysrhythmic options: Amiodarone 300 mg in 20mL NS IV/IO; Repeat once in 3-5 minutes at 150 mg in 20mL NS IV/IO. Lidocaine 1.0-1.5 mg/kg IV/IO; Repeat every 3-5 minutes to maximum of 3 mg/kg IV/IO. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC Magnesium Sulfate 2 gm IV/IO diluted in 20mL NS and administered IVSP over 1-2 minutes (for Torsades De Pointes only). MED MATH: supplied as 5Gm/10mL -> Draw up 2Gm Mag w/ 16mL NS in 60mL syringe for 10% solution Consider Sodium BiCarb 1mEq/kg (max 100mEq) after 10 minutes of downtime; Repeat once in 5 minutes at 0.5mEq/kg (Asystole, PEA, VF, VT). PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional medications. Consider termination of resuscitation efforts. Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Do not interrupt compressions for placement of an advanced airway. Avoid excessive ventilation. \u2022 Charge defibrillator without stopping chest compressions. Give everyone clear command. If a shockable rhythm presents ensure everyone is clear and shock. Otherwise continue compressions and dump charged defibrillator by changing energy settings. BE SURE TO CHANGE BACK TO CORRECT ENERGY SETTING FOR NEXT DOSE. \u2022 Capnography to confirm and monitor ET tube placement and to measure effectiveness of chest compressions and watch for ROSC. \u2022 Treat reversible causes concurrently per appropriate protocol. \u27a2 Hypoxemia (hyperventilate) \u27a2 Hypovolemia (fluid challenge up to 2000 ml) \u27a2 Hypoglycemia (<60 mg/dL) (Dextrose) \u27a2 Hypothermia (warm) \u27a2 Hyperkalemia/Hydrogen Ion (acidosis) (Sodium Bi-Carb 1 mEq/kg with a maximum 100 mEq) \u27a2 Pulmonary Embolism (ventilate & early advanced airway placement) \u27a2 Tension Pneumothorax (needle decompression) \u27a2 Cardiac Tamponade (fluid challenge up to 2000 ml) \u27a2 Overdose (Opiate) (2 mg Narcan as necessary. May repeat up to 6 mg)",
  "adult/management-post-resuscitation": "57 Adult Cardiac Arrest: Management/Post Resuscitation EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Continue high flow O2 via non-rebreather mask or BVM as indicated. \u2022 Acquire 4 lead and print (no interpretation). EMR STOP EMT \u2022 Acquire 12-lead (no interpretation) while en route to intercept location. \u2022 Capnography monitoring with an advanced airway in place. EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Acquire 12-lead. Normal Saline IV/IO bolus up to 2000 mL to maintain blood pressure of 90 systolic (monitor lung sounds) AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC \u2022 Treat dysrhythmias per appropriate protocol For hypotension not responsive to fluid: Dopamine 2-20 mcg/kg/min IV/IO infusion (pre-mixed bag or via buretrol w/ added Dopamine MED MATH: DOPAMINE: Add one vial (200mg/5mL) Dopamine to 120mL NS in a buretrol for a total of 200mg/125mL = 1600mcg/mL; Drip rate is figured same as with pre-mixed bag PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional Fluids. . Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Contact Medical Control to tailor the treatment to the patient\u2019s specific needs. \u2022 Vital signs should be recorded every 5 minutes on critical patients. \u2022 Even mild hypotension should be avoided because it can impair recovery of cerebral function. It is appropriate to administer a fluid bolus unless it is known with certainty that the patient has volume overload. \u2022 Symptomatic Bradycardia \u2013 reassess ventilations and airway, if profound bradycardia persists after reassessment; consider Cardiac Dysrhythmia/Bradycardia Protocol. \u2022 DO NOT ADMINSTER Amiodarone or Lidocaine if converted rhythm is a high degree heart block. \u2022 Paramedic Option: Placement of NG or OG tube for gastric decompression. \u2022 EMT & AEMT Option: Placement of OG tube (with any supraglottic airway in place) for gastric decompression purposes.",
  "adult/termination-of-efforts": "58 Adult Cardiac Arrest: Termination of Resuscitation EMR EMT \u2022 Resuscitative efforts for patients in cardiac arrest should not be initiated if: o The patient presents with lividity, rigor mortis, decomposition and/or injuries incompatible with life (such as decapitation). o There is a signed DNR order present. o Living Will directs to withhold resuscitative efforts. (Must have documents on scene) o Medical DPOA directs to withhold resuscitative efforts. (Must have documents on scene) \u2022 For all other patients in respiratory or cardiac arrest, the EMS provider MUST start BLS care, including defibrillation, and request a Type I assist if Type II. EMR and EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Complete standing orders appropriate to presenting rhythm. \u2022 Persistent Asystole \u2013 lasting 20 minutes (with successful ET tube or King Airway placement and IV/IO medications given.) AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS If patient is hypothermic due to inclement conditions or drowning, contact online medical control or consider transporting. Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Is scene appropriate for termination order? \u2022 Attempt to obtain a copy of the DNR or advanced directive to be attached to the chart.",
  "adult/chest-pain": "59 Adult Cardiac: Chest Pain EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Check blood glucose and treat per diabetic protocol if indicated. \u2022 Acquire 4 lead and print (no interpretation). \u2022 Aspirin: 324 mg PO EMR STOP EMT Nitro: 0.4 mg sublingual every 3-5 minutes (3 dose maximum maintaining a systolic B/P >100 mmHg). \u2022 Acquire 12-lead (no interpretation). \u2022 (Note only in a BLS setting may nitro be given prior to a 12-Lead being performed) Ondansetron ODT: For nausea 8 mg PO. May repeat once in 10 minutes. EMT STOP AEMT Cardiac monitoring. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline TKO. Consider Normal Saline bolus up to 1000 ml if BP < 90 mmHg (monitor lung sounds closely). 12-lead acquired before any medications. Nitroglycerin: 0.4 mg sublingual every 3-5 minutes as necessary maintaining a systolic B/P > 90 mmHg. Nitro-Paste: 1 inch of 2% ointment topically. o Consider: 500 ml normal saline bolus IV/IO prior to nitro (inferior wall MI). Morphine Sulfate: For pain control 2 mg IV/IO/IM/IN every 5 minutes; maximum of 10 mgs. Hydromorphone: If allergic to morphine 0.25-1 mg IV/IO/IM/IN. Repeat every 3-5 minutes as necessary. Maximum of 4 mg. OR Fentanyl 0.5-1mcg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN with max initial dose of 100mcg; Repeat in 5min PRN at \u00bd initial dose, up to a total dose of 3mcg/kg Ondansetron: For nausea 4 mg IV/IM/IO. May repeat once in 5 minutes. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC For recurrent hypotension up to an additional 1000 ml normal saline remaining aware of fluid overload. \u2022 Follow appropriate protocol for rhythm changes. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Any treatment outside of protocol Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Notify receiving hospital as soon as possible of a STEMI alert. \u2022 Monitor B/P closely when administering nitro to patients showing signs of an inferior wall MI. \u2022 Do not delay transport. \u2022 Serial 12-leads on all suspected active MI patients or any suspicious cardiac related chest pain patients. \u2022 STEMI Alerts transported to Wesley, Via Christi St Francis, or Kansas Medical Center only.",
  "adult/dysrhythmia-bradycardia": "60 Adult Cardiac: Dysrhythmia/Bradycardia EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via NC or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non- rebreather. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead and print (no interpretation). EMR STOP EMT \u2022 Acquire 12-Lead (no interpretation). EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac monitoring. \u2022 Acquire 12-lead. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline TKO. Normal Saline 500 ml bolus if systolic BP is < 90 mmHg (monitor for fluid overload). Morphine Sulfate: 2 mg every 5 minutes IV/IO or 5 mg IM/IN every 10 minutes, maximum 10 mg. \u25aa SBP must maintain > 100mmHg. Dilaudid/Hydromorphone (if allergic to Morphine) 0.25-1 mg IV/IO/IM/IN every 5 minutes as necessary to a maximum of 4 mg. OR Fentanyl 0.5-1mcg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN with max initial dose of 100mcg; Repeat in 5min PRN at \u00bd initial dose, up to a total dose of 3mcg/kg AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC \u2022 Repeat Normal Saline bolus (monitor for fluid overload). \u2022 Atropine (if symptomatic and BP < 90 with a HR < 60): 0.5mg IV/ IO. May repeat Atropine as necessary to maximum total dose of 3 mg. \u2022 Transcutaneous pacing starting at 60 bpm for symptomatic patients or those with high degree heart block or bradycardia refractory to atropine. \u2022 Consider sedation prior to pacing: Midazolam 2 mg IV/IO or 5 mg IM/IN titrated to effect. Repeat as necessary up to a maximum of 10 mg. Lorazepam (if allergic to Midazolam) 1-4 mg IVSP/IO or 2 mg IM/IN. Repeat as necessary to maximum of 4 mg. IV/IO do not exceed 2 mg/min. IV/IO must be diluted w/ equal amount normal saline. Dopamine 2-20 mcg/kg/min IV/IO infusion (only if refractory to the above treatments) MED MATH: Add one vial (200mg/5mL) Dopamine to 120mL NS in a buretrol for a total of 200mg/125mL = 1600mcg/mL; Drip rate is figured same as with pre-mixed bag Epinephrine push-dose pressor 10-20mcg every 2 min, titrated to MAP>65mmHg MED MATH: Prepare 10mcg/mL (1:100,000) Epi by adding 1mL of 1:10,000 Epi (0.1mg/mL) to 9mL NS; Administer 10-20mcg boluses (1-2mL) every 2 minutes PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Early physician contact. \u2022 With serious signs and symptoms (decreased LOC, hypotension, CHF/pulmonary edema) do not delay transcutaneous pacing. \u2022 Use EXTREME caution with atropine in patients with bradycardia caused by AMI. Atropine can exacerbate the AMI or cause VF or VT. \u2022 Do NOT use Atropine in high degree heart blocks.",
  "adult/dysrhythmia-tachycardia-with-pulse": "61 Adult Cardiac: Dysrhythmia / Tachycardia with a Pulse EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor 4-Lead only may be applied and printed (No interpretation of ECG rhythm) EMR STOP EMT \u2022 12-lead (no interpretation) EMT STOP AEMT Cardiac Monitor. \u2022 Acquire 12-lead. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline TKO. Normal Saline 500 ml bolus if systolic BP is <90 mmHg and if no signs and symptoms of fluid overload exist. \u2022 Identify and treat possible causes. \u2022 Unstable rhythms require synchronized cardioversion: \u2022 Narrow, Regular QRS: 1st 50J, 2nd 100J, 3rd 150J, 4th 200J \u2022 Narrow, Irregular QRS: 1st 100J, 2nd 150J, 3rd 200J \u2022 Wide, Regular QRS: 1st 100J, 2nd 150J, 3rd 200J \u2022 Wide, Irregular QRS: 1st 100J, 2nd 150J, 3rd 200J AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC \u2022 If patient is stable use medications first, then perform synchronized cardioversion if refractory to medications. \u2022 Narrow, Regular QRS (e.g. SVT): Adenosine 6mg Fast IV/IO followed by 10mL NS flush; Repeat in 2min with 12mg PRN \u2022 Narrow, Irregular QRS (e.g. A-Fib, A-Flutter, MAT) If symptomatic, synchronized cardiovert at appropriate dose \u2022 Wide, Regular QRS (e.g. V-Tach, SVT/A-Fib/A-Flutter w/ aberrancy): Amiodarone 150mg IV/IO over 10 min; May repeat in 10 min PRN; (See MED MATH below) Lidocaine 1-1.5mg/kg IV/IO every 5min to max of 3mg/kg \u2022 Wide, Irregular QRS (e.g. A-Fib w/ aberrancy, WPW w/ A-Fib, MAT, Polymorphic VT/Torsades de Pointes) Torsades: Magnesium 1-2g IV/IO of 50% solution diluted in 20mL NS (to make 10% solution) over 10min (2mL/min) (See MED MATH below) Amiodarone 150mg IV/IO over 10 min; May repeat in 10 min PRN; (See MED MATH below) \u2022 Consider sedation prior to cardioversion: Midazolam 2 mg IV/IO or 5 mg IM/IN titrated to effect. Repeat as necessary up to a maximum of 10 mg. Lorazepam (if allergic to Midazolam) 1-4 mg IVSP/IO or 2 mg IM/IN. Repeat as necessary to a maximum of 4 mg. IV/IO do not exceed 2 mg/min. IV/IO must be diluted w/ equal amount normal saline. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Repeat synchronized cardioversion after fourth attempt. Key Points/Considerations \u2022 MEDICATION MATH: Amiodarone infusion: Draw up 150mg Amiodarone into 10mL NS and administer at 1mL/min. Hold onto the syringe plunger while infusing to prevent backflow; will need to be given with fluids to allow for flushing between doses. \u2022 MEDICATION MATH: Magnesium sulfate: Draw up 2Gr Mag w/ 16mL NS for 10% solution and give 2mL/min; will take 10 min. to give 62",
  "adult/abdominal-pain-discomfort": "64 Adult Medical: Abdominal Pain/Abdominal Discomfort EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac Monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. Ondansetron (Zofran) for nausea as needed 4 mg IV/IO/IM; May repeat once in 5 minutes. Morphine 2 mg IV/IO as necessary every 5 minutes up to 10 mg OR IM/IN 5 mg; Repeat once in 10 minutes. If Morphine is unavailable: Fentanyl 0.5-1 mcg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN with max initial dose of 100mcg; Repeat in 5 minutes PRN at \u00bd initial dose, up to total dose of 3mcg/kg Dilaudid 0.25 mg \u2013 1 mg IV/IO/IM/IN every 5 minutes as necessary up to 4 mg Fentanyl 0.5-1mcg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN with max initial dose of 100mcg; Repeat in 5min PRN at \u00bd initial dose, up to a total dose of 3mcg/kg AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC Toradol 30mg IV/IO/IN or 60mg IM (Geriatric age 65+ is \u00bd the dose for both) PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Begin transport as soon as possible. \u2022 Transport patient in a position of comfort if possible. \u2022 Use caution palpating patients with possible cardiac involvement, i.e. aortic aneurysms. \u2022 EMR and EMT may apply 4 \u2013 lead ECG if indicated. No Interpretation. \u2022 SPO2 and cardiac monitoring required with any narcotic administration.",
  "adult/abuse-neglect": "65 Adult Medical: Abuse / Neglect EMR EMT AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Ensure that scene is secure. \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Initiate treatment as necessary \u2022 If possible remove patient from scene, transporting to hospital even if there is no medical reason for transport. \u2022 If patient is in immediate danger, let law enforcement handle scene. \u2022 AVOID ACCUSATIONS as this may delay transport. Adult with child may not be the abuser. \u2022 Never communicate via radio abuse suspicions. \u2022 Carefully document findings and report to physicians at the hospital. \u2022 Immediately contact the appropriate law enforcement agency to file a report. Make sure to include this in your documentation of the call. EMR, EMT, AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 EMS care providers are MANDATORY REPORTERS by law with the duty to notify authorities of suspected child abuse. \u2022 Abuse/neglect are widespread enough that nearly all emergency responders will see these problems at some time. The first step in recognizing abuse or neglect is to accept that they exist and to learn the signs and symptoms: \u27a2 Noticeable injuries not mentioned in the history. \u27a2 History does not explain physical findings of the injury. \u27a2 Injuries with a pattern: o Bite marks o Cigarette Burns o Rope (circumferential or linear marks at the ankles or wrists) o Loop scars (doubled-over electrical wire or telephone cord) o Belt marks o Etc. \u27a2 Injuries to genital or perineal areas. \u27a2 Multiple repetitive unexplained injuries.",
  "adult/altered-mental-status-unresponsive": "66 Adult Medical: Altered Mental Status/ Unresponsive EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Spinal motion restriction if trauma suspected. \u2022 Check blood glucose level. (See Diabetic Protocol) \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation). \u2022 Determine and treat causes per appropriate protocol. EMR STOP EMT Acquire 12-lead (no interpretation). EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC Cardiac Monitor IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. Up to 1000 ml Normal Saline bolus with systolic B/P 90 mmHg or less and no signs or symptoms of fluid overload exist. \u2022 Determine and treat causes per appropriate protocol. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Consult Medical Control as soon as possible. \u2022 IO access should be attempted if no peripheral access can be obtained after 2 attempts or 90 seconds in patients with cardiovascular collapse. \u2022 Potential causes of altered mental status: \u27a2 A \u2013 Alcohol \u27a2 E \u2013 Epilepsy, Endocrine, Electrolytes \u27a2 I \u2013 Insulin \u27a2 O \u2013 Opiates and other drugs \u27a2 U \u2013 Uremia \u27a2 T \u2013 Temperature, Trauma \u27a2 I \u2013 Infection \u27a2 P \u2013 Psychogenic, Poisoning \u27a2 S \u2013 Shock, Stroke, Space-occupying lesion, Subarachnoid hemorrhage \u2022 Questions to consider: \u27a2 What was patient doing prior to this? \u27a2 Has it happened before?",
  "adult/anaphylaxis-allergic-reactions": "67 Anaphylaxis/ Allergic Reactions EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. o Consider ventilator assistance for severe dyspnea or hypoventilation. \u2022 Determine cause of reaction, time of exposure, and amount of substance patient was exposed to if possible. o Remove patient from source or source from patient if still present. o For Moderate Allergic Reaction (e.g., hives, itching, redness, stable vitals) administer: Diphenhydramine 50 mg PO For Anaphylaxis administer: Epinephrine 1:1,000 dose 0.3 mg IM used first for shock states; Repeat x1 in 5 minutes as necessary. EMR STOP EMT Albuterol 2.5 mg in 3 mL (unit dose) + Ipratropium 0.5 mg in 2.5 mL (unit dose) mixed together via nebulizer for wheezing. May repeat as necessary. EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac Monitor as indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline TKO. Normal Saline 500 mL fluid bolus. Repeat one time as necessary to maintain BP > 90. Diphenhydramine 25 \u2013 50 mg IV/IO/IM. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC If Epinephrine IM is unsuccessful after 2 doses, administer 0.5mg 1:10,000 I/IO Methylprednisolone (Solu-Medrol) 125 mg IV/IO. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 \u2022 S/S of allergic reaction: gradual onset, mild flushing, rash, or hives, mild bronchoconstriction, and normal mental status. \u2022 S/S of anaphylaxis: sudden onset, severe flushing, rash or hives, severe bronchoconstriction, laryngospasm, anxiety, sense of impending doom, may decrease to confusion then unconsciousness. \u2022 Severe reactions may develop within 2-20 minutes following exposure.",
  "adult/dehydration": "68 Adult Medical: Dehydration EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation). EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC Cardiac Monitor Acquire 12-Lead \u2022 IV/IO access. \u2022 With signs and symptoms of dehydration present: o Patient must have tachycardia >120 bpm and at least one additional sign or symptom: \u27a2 Dry mucous membranes \u27a2 Skin tinting \u27a2 Hypotension \u27a2 Decreased LOC related to suspected dehydration \u27a2 High Fever \u27a2 Severe vomiting or diarrhea \u27a2 Suspected heat related emergency \u27a2 Pointed and peaked T-waves \u27a2 Pale or clammy skin Normal Saline bolus 1000 mL; May repeat x1 if patient remains tachycardic. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Monitor lung sounds closely throughout bolus. \u2022 Monitor SPO2. \u2022 Excessive rapid fluid replacement can cause an imbalance in electrolytes and other lab values. This may increase the patient\u2019s stay at the hospital. With this said, minor dehydration will not be treated in the field. \u2022 If the patient is on a beta blocker medication, consider the heart rate may be less than 120 bpm; look for additional S/S of dehydration.",
  "adult/diabetic-emergencies": "69 Adult Medical: Diabetic Emergencies EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor 4-lead only maybe applied and printed (no interpretation). \u2022 Check blood glucose level. If blood glucose is known or suspected to be low and patient is able to self-administer and swallow on command, give oral glucose one unit dose (24 gms). Repeat one time as necessary. EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Cardiac Monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. \u2022 Hyperglycemia: Blood glucose is above 250 mg/dL. Normal Saline bolus up to 2000 mL IV/IO. \u2022 Patients presenting with signs and symptoms of hypoglycemia: Dextrose 25 gms IV/IO. May repeat in 10 minutes if signs and symptoms of hypoglycemia are present. Follow Dextrose administration w/100mL normal saline infusion. If unable to obtain IV access, administer Glucagon 1 mg IM. IO administration only if IM Glucagon is unsuccessful. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 DKA has slow onset lasting 12-24 hours. S/S include polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia, warm, dry skin and mucous membranes, nausea/vomiting abdominal pain, tachycardia, Kussmaul\u2019s respirations, fruity breath odor, decreased mental function, or coma. \u2022 If the patient wishes to refuse transport: \u27a2 Patient is alert. \u27a2 Blood glucose greater than 60 mg/dl. \u27a2 No other complaints or complications. \u27a2 Instructed to eat complex carbohydrates, contact physician, and to recheck blood glucose regularly.",
  "adult/nausea-and-or-vomiting": "70 Adult Medical: Nausea and/or Vomiting EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead. (no interpretation) EMR STOP EMT Ondansetron (Zofran) ODT 8mg PO. Repeat once in 10 minutes if needed. EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC Cardiac Monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO Ondansetron (Zofran) 4 mg IV/IO/IM if needed. Repeat once in 5 minutes. See Dehydration Protocol for suspected dehydration. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 See Dehydration protocol for severe cases.",
  "adult/ob-gyn-childbirth": "71 Adult Medical: OB/GYN: Childbirth EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 If OB (>20 weeks gestation) and patient is a trauma patient place on left side, maintaining neutral spine. \u2022 Assess for delivery, crowning. \u2022 If childbirth is imminent, prepare for delivery. o Position patient in manageable position for delivery. o Support baby\u2019s head over the perineum. If membranes cover the baby\u2019s head as it is delivered, tear the sac as gently as possible to allow for amniotic fluid to escape. Make note of any meconium in amniotic fluid. o Once head has been fully presented release any nuchal cords and suction oropharynx and then nostrils. \u27a2 If a nuchal cord is present after the head has delivered clamp the cord with two clamps and cut cord. A nuchal cord does not allow proper oxygenation to the baby. o Gently guide the head downward until the shoulder appears and the other shoulder should present with a little upward guidance. o Once baby has fully been delivered keep baby at same level as vagina and clamp the cord at 7 and 5 inches from base and cut between clamps. o Assess APGAR score. \u2022 If childbirth is breech: o Support extremities or buttocks until back appears. o Once back has presented grasp iliac wings and apply gentle downward pressure, DOT NOT PULL. Gently swing infant in path of least resistance, anteriorly and posteriorly. Both shoulders should appear posteriorly. o Splint the humerus next to the body with your fingers and gently pull traction. o Once torso has delivered, swing legs upward until baby is vertical. This will assist in delivery of the head. o Follow normal delivery of infant. \u2022 If there is a prolapsed cord or limb presentation: o Place mother in prone, head to knee, position. o Place a sterile gloved hand in the vagina and attempt to hold the baby\u2019s head away from the cord. o Keep the cord moist with a sterile dressing and sterile water. o Transport as soon as possible. EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Monitor EKG if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. Consider 1000 mL fluid bolus IV/IO post-delivery. May repeat x1 if patient remains hypoperfused Ondansetron (Zofran) for nausea 4 mg IV/IO. Repeat once in 5 minutes. \u2022 Manage airway as appropriate for the infant. If meconium is present, use meconium aspirator to suction the airway. \u2022 Treat OB patient seizures per Seizure protocol OR Pre-eclampsia/Eclampsia protocol as appropriate AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Contact medical control as soon as possible for OB complications. 72 Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Transport as soon as possible. \u2022 Assess OB patients for estimated date of delivery, Para (live births), Gravida (been pregnant), expected complications, multiple births, and pre- natal care. \u2022 REMEMBER once baby has been delivered to dry, warm, suction, and stimulate. Newborns are very susceptible to hypothermia. \u2022 When suctioning meconium use no more than 100 mmHg of suction. \u2022 All afterbirth tissues need to be transported to the hospital with patient for exam. \u2022 Gently massage the uterus after birth and allow infant to suckle as this will assist in placenta delivery. \u2022 The APGAR should be done at 1 and 5 minute intervals. \u2022 APGAR SCORE CARD: SIGN 0 1 2 A Apperance Blue, pale Body pink, limbs blue Completely pink P Pulse Absent Below 100 Above 100 G Grimace No Response Grimace Vigorous cry A Activity Limp Some Flexion Active motion R Respirations No effort Weak, irregular Strong cry \u2022 If pre-term labor (24-27 weeks), do not delay transport. Transport as soon as possible and give supportive care. \u2022 EMR and EMT may assist paramedic in applying 3 or 4 \u2013 lead ECG if indicated.",
  "adult/ob-gyn-pre-eclampsia-eclampsia": "73 Adult Medical: OB/GYN: Pre-Eclampsia/Eclampsia EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor (4-lead) only may be applied and printed (no interpretation). EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Monitor EKG if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO Ondansetron (Zofran) for nausea IV/IO 4 mg. Repeat once in 5 minutes. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC \u2022 Severe features of Pre-Eclampsia: \u2022 SBP>140 OR DBP>90 and at least one of the following symptoms: \u25aa Headache \u25aa Mental confusion \u25aa Visual changes/disturbances \u25aa Right Upper Quadrant or Epigastric pain \u25aa Pulmonary edema Treatment for Pre-Eclampsia: Magnesium sulfate 4gm IV/IO (20% solution) administered over 20 minutes o MED MATH: Using 60mL syringe, draw up 4gm/8mL Magnesium and then draw up 12mL NS and mix -> administer at 1mL/min \u2022 Eclampsia = pre-eclampsia symptoms plus seizures \u2022 Treatment for Eclampsia: \u25aa Magnesium Sulfate 4gm IV/IO (50% solution) administered over 10-20 minutes \u2022 MED MATH: DO NOT DILUTE: Using a 10mL syringe, draw up 4gm/8mL in 10mL syringe and give 1mL every 2 minutes (will take 16 minutes total to administer) \u25aa If seizures persist after 2-3 minutes since starting Magnesium infusion: \u2022 Lorazepam 1-4mg IVSP/IO or 2mg Deep IM/IN up to maximum dose of 4mg o IV/IO do not exceed 2mg/min o Must be diluted in equal amount of normal saline to give IV/IO; IM do not dilute, give deep into muscle mass \u2022 Midazolam 2-5mg IV/IO/IM/IN; max single dose 5mg; May repeat one time \u2022 BE AWARE OF RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional Magnesium (if available) or for continued seizures despite administration of benzodiazepine If imminent delivery is possible, contact medical control BEFORE administration of magnesium as it is contraindicated 2 hours immediately preceding delivery Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Transport as soon as possible. \u2022 Mag toxicity (progressive): hypotension -> loss of deep tendon reflexes (check patellar reflexes often) -> somnolence, slurred speech -> respiratory depression/paralysis -> cardiac arrest \u2022 Treatment for Mag Toxicity: Stop magnesium infusion, support respiratory effort \u2022 Placental delivery is the only definitive management for pre-eclampsia and eclampsia therefore must be aggressive and early with interventions/treatments",
  "adult/overdose-toxic-exposure": "74 Adult Medical: Overdose or Toxic Exposure EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather \u2022 Determine what was taken, when, and how much \u2022 Check blood glucose level \u2022 Cardiac Monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation) \u2022 Suspected Opiate Overdose - If symptomatic (unresponsive with respirations less than 10/min or signs of inadequate perfusion, pinpoint pupils): Naloxone (Narcan) 2mg IM repeat in 5 minutes as necessary to a maximum of 4 mg. Cardiac Arrest Dose: 2mg IM repeat in 5 minutes as necessary to a maximum of 4 mg. EMR STOP EMT \u2022 Apply capnography (normal reading 35-45 mmHg) \u2022 Ingested non-caustic poisoning within 1 hour or less: (patient must be alert enough to control their own airway) Activated Charcoal 50 gms PO \u2022 May perform 12-lead (no interpretation) EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac Monitor \u2022 Acquire 12-lead \u2022 Monitor capnography \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO Symptomatic opiate overdose: Naloxone (Narcan) 0.5 \u2013 1mg IV/IO or 2 mg IM/IN (if all other routes are unavailable) every 5 min as necessary to a maximum dose of 6 mg Cardiac Arrest Dose: 2 mg IV/IO AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC \u2022 Organophosphate poisoning: Atropine 2 mg IV/IO every 3 \u2013 5 minutes until secretions dry \u2022 Dystonic reaction: Diphenhydramine (Benadryl) 25 \u2013 50 mg IV/IO/IM \u2022 TCA overdose: Sodium Bicarbonate 1 mEq/kg IV/IO over 2 minutes \u2022 Beta blocker OD: Glucagon 1 mg IV/ IO \u2022 Calcium channel blocker OD: Glucagon 1 mg IV/ IO PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional antidotes as needed. 75 Key Points/Considerations \u2022 If toxicity is uncertain or multiple substances are involved contact a physician prior to pharmacological intervention. \u2022 Includes patients who are unconscious/unresponsive without suspected trauma or other causes, and patients with a brief loss of consciousness. \u2022 Dystonic reaction is uncontrolled contractions of face, neck, or tongue. \u2022 Monitor patients with TCA overdose closely for rapid changes and deterioration and changes in their condition. \u2022 Obtain and give to ER staff all meds/containers found. \u2022 Obtain what meds were taken (pill bottles), number of pills taken, time of ingestion and if vomiting occurred. \u2022 S/S of Phenothiazine overdose: (Anti-psychotics including Compro, Prolixin,Thorazine, Compazine, and others) \u27a2 Tardive Dyskinesia (involuntary movements of the facial muscles and tongue) and acute dystonic reactions. \u2022 S/S of TCA overdose: \u27a2 Widened QRS >0.10 \u27a2 Tachycardia >120 bpm \u27a2 Hypotension \u2022 S/S of Organophosphate poisoning: \u27a2 Muscle fasciculation \u27a2 Diarrhea \u27a2 Wheezing \u27a2 Abdominal cramping \u27a2 Salivation \u27a2 Seizures \u27a2 Altered mental status \u27a2 Pinpoint pupils \u27a2 DUMBELS: Diarrhea, urination, miosis, bradycardia, bronchospasm, bronchorrhea, emesis, lacrimation, salivation, sweating, secretions, seizures. \u27a2 SLUDGE: Salivation, lacrimation, urination, diarrhea, GI cramps, emesis. \u2022 S/S of atropinization: \u27a2 Pupillary dilation \u27a2 Tachycardia \u27a2 Flushing \u27a2 Diminished diaphoresis \u27a2 Drying of secretions Calcium Blockers TCA Beta Blockers Organophosphates Verapamil Amitriptyline Inderal Disulfoton Cardiazem Amoxapine Lopressor Tetraethyl Pyrophosphate Nifedipine Clomipramine Tenormin Parathion Nicardipine Desipramine Corgard Phosdrin Nimodipine Dothiepin Blocadren Phorate Felodipine Imipramine Cartrol Doumaphos Amlodipine Lofepramine Visken Chlorpytigos Bepridil Maprotiline Sectral Trichlorfon Flunarizine Nortriptyline Normodyne Ronnel Isradipine Protriptyline Betapace Diazinon Nitrendipine Trimipramine Zebeta Malathion List is not all inclusive.",
  "adult/pain-management": "76 Adult Medical: Pain Management EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. EMR STOP EMT Acetaminophen 640mg/20mL oral solution PO (160mg/5mL) or two 325 mg (650mg) tablets PO EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac Monitor. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. Ondansetron (Zofran) 4 mg IV/IM/IO for nausea. Repeat as necessary once in 5 minutes. \u2022 Utilize one of the following as necessary: Morphine 2 mg IV/IO as necessary every 5 minutes maximum 10 mg (burns maximum dose is 20 mg.) OR IM/IN 5 mg Repeat once in 10 minutes. Fentanyl 0.5-1mcg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN with max initial dose of 100mcg; Repeat in 5min PRN at \u00bd initial dose, up to a total dose of 3mcg/kg Dilaudid 0.25 mg \u2013 1 mg IV/IO/IM/IN every 5 minutes as necessary. Maximum 4 mg. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC \u2022 Toradol 30mg IV/IO/IN or 60mg IM (Geriatric 65+ is \u00bd the dose). o May be used in conjunction with narcotic. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional doses of pain medication Key Points/Considerations \u2022 For patients with: \u27a2 Severe burns without hemodynamic compromise. \u27a2 Isolated extremity injuries such as fractures or dislocations with severe pain. \u27a2 Suspected hip fractures should be treated as extremity injuries. \u27a2 Shoulder injuries should be treated as extremity injuries. \u27a2 Kidney Stones \u27a2 Abdominal pains- stable and unstable. \u27a2 Back Pains \u27a2 Multi-systems trauma (without suspected head injury) \u2022 For all other painful conditions, providers must consult medical control for orders. \u2022 Contraindications to standing order pain management: altered mental status secondary to a suspected head injury, hypoventilation, hypoperfusion. \u2022 Use caution and risk/benefits ratio with severely intoxicated/obtunded patients with traumatic injuries. \u2022 SPO2 and cardiac monitoring required with any narcotic administration. \u2022 Acetaminophen: Ensure pt has not had Tylenol, acetaminophen-containing medications such as cold medicines or narcotics within the last 4 hours",
  "adult/acute-agitation-excited-delirium": "77 Adult Medical: Acute Agitation/ Excited Delirium Syndrome EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and Vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Check Blood Glucose. If not within normal limits treat per Diabetic Protocol. \u2022 Physical restraints as needed (If possible, strongly consider deferring until chemical restraint is applied) \u2022 Cardiac Monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation) EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac Monitor \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline normal saline only after chemical and physical restraints. 1000 CC normal saline if excited delirium is suspected. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC Haloperidol 5mg IM (if older than 65y/o give 2.5mg IM) o Utilize one of the following in conjunction with Haloperidol: Midazolam 5 mg IM Repeat once as necessary every 5-10 minutes up to 10 mg. Lorazepam 2 mg IM Repeat once as necessary in 10 minutes to maximum of 4 mg. o IM do not dilute, give injection deep into muscle mass. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional Lorazepam Additional Midazolam Additional IV normal saline bolus Key Points/Considerations \u2022 If patient is physically restrained, chemical restraints should be strongly considered. \u2022 Be aware for malignment hyperthermia in excited delirium patients, use aggressive external cooling measures. \u2022 Repeat vitals every 5 minutes. \u2022 Under no circumstances are patients to be restrained in the prone position. \u2022 See restraint procedures. \u2022 SPO2,cardiac monitoring, and end tital CO2 monitoring is required with any chemical restraint administration. \u2022 Consider all possible medical/trauma causes for patient behavior. \u2022 If patient in ecited delirium suffers cardiac arrest, consider fluid bolus and sodium bicarbonate early.",
  "adult/respiratory-asthma-copd": "78 Adult Respiratory Emergencies: Asthma/Bronchospasms/COPD EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Monitor SPO2 (obtain room air saturation if possible, prior to oxygen) \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Check blood glucose. If out of normal limits treat per Diabetic Protocol. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation). EMR STOP EMT \u2022 Cardiac monitor: Acquire 12-lead (no interpretation) \u2022 Determine if patient has been given their own asthma medications. Albuterol 2.5 mg in 3 mL (unit dose) + Ipratropium 0.5 mg in 2.5 mL (unit dose) mixed, via nebulizer. Repeat, as necessary. EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac Monitor: Acquire 12-lead. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. \u2022 Medication options: AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC Magnesium Sulfate 2 gms IV/IO over 20 minutes. Dilute into a 10% solution o MED MATH: Draw up 2Gm/4mL Mag w/ 16mL NS into 60mL syringe for 10% solution; Administer 1mL/min Methylprednisolone (Solu-Medrol) 125 mg IV/IO Consider 0.5-1 mg Lorazepam IV/IO/IM/IN OR 2mg Midazolam IV/IO/IM/IN for extreme anxiety to aid patient in accepting the CPAP mask. Epinephrine 1:1,000 dose 0.3 mg IM; Use with EXTREME caution in patients with cardiac history PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Repeat Epinephrine 1:1,000 dose 0.3 mg IM or for order for 1:10,000 dose 0.5 mg IV/IO. Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Position patient sitting or in position of comfort. \u2022 Absence of breath sounds can be indicative of status asthmaticus. Be prepared for imminent respiratory arrest. \u2022 Presence of wheezing is not only an indicator of Asthma/COPD. Consider pulmonary edema, pneumonia, upper airway obstruction, anaphylaxis, and acute allergic bronchospasm. \u2022 Epinephrine should be reserved for those who are unable to generate adequate tidal volume to deliver aerosolized drug to the bronchial tree. \u2022 Draft nebulizer with BVM \u2022 CPAP: follow CPAP procedure. \u2022 End-tidal CO2 nasal cannula. \u2022 Medication intervention other than oxygen - patient must be transported. \u2022 Monitor heart rate closely. When administering Albuterol, contact a physician if heart rate increases to >150 bpm.",
  "adult/respiratory-pulmonary-edema-chf": "79 Adult Respiratory Emergencies: Pulmonary Edema/CHF EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Monitor SPO2 (obtain room air saturation if possible prior to oxygen). \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Place patient in fowler\u2019s position. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation). EMR STOP EMT \u2022 CPAP if tolerated and meets indications. \u2022 Acquire 12-lead (no interpretation) EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac Monitor. \u2022 Acquire 12-lead. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. Nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingual (prior to CPAP application) if B/P is > 100 mmHg systolic. Repeat every 3 \u2013 5 minutes Nitro-Paste 1-inch of 2% ointment topically. Morphine Sulfate (for chest pain) at 2 mg IV/IO over 1 minutes or 5mg IM/IN. Repeat in 5 minutes as necessary to a total dose of 10 mg. \u2022 Initial systolic B/P must be above 120 mmHg. \u2022 Patient must be conscious with adequate ventilatory effort. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC Furosemide (Lasix) 40 mg IVSP/IO max given over at least two minutes o 1mg/kg if already prescribed Lasix. Consider administering Lorazepam 0.5-1 mg IV/IO/IN/IM OR 2mg Midazolam IV/IO/IM/IN to assist relieving anxiety of the CPAP mask. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional medications. Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Position patient in fowler\u2019s position. \u2022 CPAP: follow CPAP procedure. \u2022 Capnography can be monitored while using CPAP; Ensure applying prior to placement of CPAP mask. \u2022 Monitor B/P, pulse oximetry and signs of respiratory depression.",
  "adult/seizures": "80 Adult Medical: Seizures EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12- 15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Check blood glucose. If out of normal limits treat per Diabetic Protocol. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation EMR STOP EMT Acetaminophen 640mg/20mL PO (160mg/5mL or 2/ 325mg tablets total of 650mg) WHEN FEVER IS PRESENT (TEMP >100.4\u00b0F OR 38\u00b0C and is alert and can manage own airway) EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Cardiac monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. Administer one of the following within 3 minutes of seizure activity. Lorazepam 1 \u2013 4 IVSP/IO or 2 mg deep IM/IN as necessary up to a maximum dose 4 mg. o IV/IO not to exceed 2 mg/minute o Must be diluted in an equal amount of Normal Saline to give IV/IO. o IM do not dilute, give injection deep into muscle mass. Midazolam 2-5 mg IV/IO/IM/IN maximum single dose of 5 mg. May repeat one time. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional benzodiazepine doses Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Do not delay medication administration. Consider IN or IM prior to IV access. \u2022 Protect the patient and EMS crew from injury during the seizure. \u2022 If seizure d/t Eclampsia, see OB/GYN Pre-eclampsia/Eclampsia Protocol \u2022 Acetaminophen: Ensure pt has not had total dose of >650mg within the last 4 hours (e.g. Tylenol, acetaminophen-containing medications such as cold medicines, narcotics)",
  "adult/sepsis": "81 Adult Medical: Sepsis EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Check blood glucose level if equipped. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (No interpretation). \u2022 Obtain temperature. EMR STOP EMT \u2022 Acquire 12-lead (No interpretation). \u2022 Apply/monitor EtCO2 capnography. Acetaminophen 640mg/20mL oral soliton PO (160mg/5mL) or 2/325 mg (650mg) tablets PO WHEN FEVER IS PRESENT (TEMP >100.4\u00b0F OR 38\u00b0C) EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Acquire/Interpret 12-lead ECG. \u2022 Large-bore IV access or IO if unable to establish IV access AS SOON AS POSSIBLE. Normal Saline infusion at rate of 30mL/kg/hr in boluses using pressure bag. o May repeat x4 if no pulmonary edema up to max of 2000mL. o Boluses may be given in rapid succession if SBP <90mmHg. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC BEFORE STARTING PRESSOR AGENTS, MUST HAVE ENTIRE FLUID BOLUS OF 30ML/KG INFUSED Dopamine infusion 2-20 mcg/kg/min IV/IO if hypotensive refractory to normal saline infusions. Titrate to effect. MED MATH: Add one vial (200mg/5mL) Dopamine to 120mL NS in a buretrol for a total of 200mg/125mL = 1600mcg/mL; Drip rate is figured same as with pre-mixed bag Epinephrine push-dose pressor 10-20mcg every 2 min, titrated to MAP>65mmHg MED MATH: Prepare 10mcg/mL (1:100,000) Epi by adding 1mL of 1:10,000 Epi (0.1mg/mL) to 9mL NS; Administer 10-20mcg boluses (1-2mL) every 2 minutes PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Focus on rapid intervention, IV hydration, and early notification of concern for potential sepsis pt. \u2022 Concern for any new or worsening infection can include: reported fever, shaking chills, sweatiness, new cough, difficulty or less than usual urination, unexplained or new AMS, flushed skin, pallor, new rash or mottling. \u2022 Vital signs should be obtained every 5 minutes. \u2022 Acetaminophen: Ensure pt has not had total dose of >650mg within the last 4 hours (e.g. Tylenol, acetaminophen-containing medications such as cold medicines, narcotics)",
  "adult/stroke": "82 Adult Medical: Stroke EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Check blood glucose. If out of normal limits treat per Diabetic Protocol. \u2022 Determine at what point signs and symptoms appeared. \u2022 If onset of symptoms is less than 6 hours or a \u201cwake up stroke,\u201d notify appropriate stroke center (Via Christi St Francis or Wesley Medical Center). Transport immediately. \u2022 If onset is within 24 hours patient should be taken to a stroke center (Via Christi St. Francis or Wesley Medical Center) as a non-stroke alert patient. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation). EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Monitor EKG. \u2022 Acquire 12-lead. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. If tachycardic and blood pressure is < 90 systolic, fluid bolus up to 1000 cc Normal Saline IV to maintain blood pressure above 90. \u2022 Secure airway as needed. \u2022 Treat seizure per seizure protocol. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Contact Medical Control as soon as possible to give Stroke Alert. \u2022 Limit scene time. \u2022 Repeat vitals every 5 minutes if patient meets stroke criteria. \u2022 All strokes that are within the 6 hour or \u201cwake up stroke\u201d time limit are Code Red. Transport regular traffic unless patient is hemodynamically unstable. \u2022 Cincinnati Stroke Scale: \u2022 Speech \u2013 repeat: \u201cYou can\u2019t teach an old dog new tricks.\u201d Correct words and no slurring. \u25aa Facial Droop \u2013 Have patient smile. \u25aa Arm Drift \u2013 Have patient close eyes and hold arms out straight for 10 seconds without unequal movement or drifting.",
  "adult/carbon-monoxide-poisoning": "84 Adult Environmental: Carbon Monoxide Poisoning EMR \u2022 Remove patient from the source. \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and high flow oxygen 15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Consider ventilatory assistance for severe dyspnea or hypoventilation. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation). EMR STOP EMT \u2022 Obtain CO reading by using the RAD 57 (Rescue 410, #420/Duty Officer vehicle, or the X-series monitor). EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Cardiac monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. \u2022 Treat seizures and cardiac dysrhythmias per protocols. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 All CO poisoning patients will be transported to Wesley Med Center for hyperbaric oxygen treatment. \u2022 All patients with suspected CO poisoning are required to have high flow O2 initiated and maintained. \u2022 S/S: dull headache, weakness, dizziness, N/V, shortness of breath, confusion, blurred vision, loss of consciousness \u2022 SpO2/Pulse oximeter will be inaccurate. \u2022 *100 percent oxygen reduces the elimination half-life of CO to 74 minutes, i.e. a SPCO 10% in theory would decrease to 5% after 74 minutes of oxygen @ 15 lpm via NRB. \u2022 **In pregnant patients the CO levels of the fetus is double of the mother\u2019s",
  "adult/hyperthermia": "85 Adult Environmental: Hyperthermia EMR EMT \u2022 Remove from heat source. \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Obtain temperature. \u2022 > 103 degrees - cool patient with ice packs to the groin, neck, and axillae. Cool water and fanning of the patient is also effective. (Remove when body temperature is 100 degrees.) \u2022 Remove clothing. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Cardiac monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation). EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Monitor EKG if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline 500 mL up to 2000 mL\u2019s with hypovolemia and hemodynamic compromise. Assess breath sounds for tolerance. Ondansetron (Zofran) for nausea 4 mg IV/IO. Repeat once in 5 minutes. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC \u2022 Treat seizures per seizure protocol. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Use caution with cooling as it can cause shivering or hypothermia. \u2022 Vigorous fluid administration may produce pulmonary edema.",
  "adult/hypothermia": "86 Adult Environmental: Hypothermia EMR EMT \u2022 Remove from cold environment and begin warming with blankets. \u2022 CAB and vital signs (assess for 30-45 seconds) \u2022 Obtain temperature. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Check blood glucose. If out of normal limits treat per Diabetic Protocol. EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 IV/IO access. Warm IV fluids up to 2000 mL \u2022 Monitor EKG if indicated. \u2022 If temperature is below 86 degrees F, CPR and one defibrillation at 200 joules if indicated until temp is above 86 degrees. \u2022 Follow CPR protocol if patient is pulseless, apneic, and temperature is above 86 degrees F, but lengthen intervals of IV/IO medications until temperature is above 93 degrees F. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Prevent further heat loss in patients < 86 degrees; temperatures > 93 degrees may be re-warmed. \u2022 Intubation should be avoided in the severely hypothermic patient with spontaneous respirations unless urgently needed using extreme caution. \u2022 All hypothermic patients triaged code blue will be transported.",
  "adult/burns": "88 Adult Trauma: Burns EMR EMT \u2022 Stop the burning process. o Remove necessary clothing leaving all stuck clothing. o Remove rings, bracelets, and constricting bands. o Flush briefly with cool water if still burning (chemical burns only) \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Maintain body temperature. \u2022 Chemical burns: irrigate with copious amounts of water or saline for 20 minutes. EXCEPTION: Phosphorus and Lime Powder -> Brush off dry powder chemicals before flushing with water. \u2022 Decontaminate all patients exposed to hazardous materials before entering the ambulance. \u2022 Elevate extremity in position of function to minimize edema, monitoring neurovascular status. \u2022 Treat for shock and hypothermia. \u2022 Cover burns with dry sterile dressings. \u2022 Cover patient with burn sheet. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation). EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Monitor EKG if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline Per ABLS Guidelines: patients age 14+ administer 500 ml/hour. Ondansetron (Zofran) for nausea 4 mg IV/IO. Repeat once in 5 minutes. Morphine Sulfate 5 mg IV/IO/IN/IM. Repeat every 3-5 minutes as necessary to a maximum of 20 mg. Morphine allergy/unavailable: Fentanyl 0.5-1mcg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN with max initial dose 100mcg every 5 minutes PRN at \u00bd initial dose, up to total dose of 3mcg/kg Morphine allergy/unavailable: Dilaudid 0.25-1 mg IV/IO/IM/IN every 3-5 minutes to a maximum of 8 mg AEMT STOP AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional narcotic pain relief Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Remove patient from exposure without compromising crew safety. \u2022 Electrical Burns: consider spinal immobilization and assess for extremity fractures. \u2022 Electrical, Flame, or scald burns should be covered with dry sterile sheets and dressings.",
  "adult/chest-abdominal-injuries": "89 Adult Trauma: Chest / Abdominal Injuries EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. o Record vital signs every 5 minutes on critical patients. \u2022 Stabilize life-threatening wounds. (Control hemorrhage, cover sucking chest wounds, etc.) \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Spinal motion restriction if indicated. \u2022 Moist sterile dressing to abdominal evisceration. \u2022 Penetrating objects should be stabilized \u2013 DO NOT REMOVE THEM. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor 4-Lead only maybe applied and printed (No interpretation) EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT Cardiac monitor. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline @ TKO or up to 2000 mL bolus to maintain systolic BP > 90. Assess lung sounds for tolerance. Ondansetron (Zofran) for nausea 4 mg IV/IO. Repeat once in 5 minutes. Morphine Sulfate for pain 2 mg IV/IO or 5mg IM/IN. Repeat as necessary every 3-5 minutes maximum 10 mg Fentanyl 0.5-1mcg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN with max initial dose of 100mcg; Repeat in 5 minutes PRN at \u00bd initial dose, up to total dose of 3mcg/kg AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC \u2022 Chest injury with tension pneumothorax. o Chest decompression with available pneumothorax kit. o Needle decompression with 14 ga catheter if pneumothorax kit is unavailable \u27a2 Code Blue Trauma \u2013 (bilateral) prior to termination of arrest. \u2022 Consider sedation in conscious patient prior to decompression. Midazolam 2 mg IV/IO or 5 mg IM/IN. \u2022 Crush injury for an extended time > 45 minutes. Consider Sodium Bicarbonate 1 mEq/kg IV/IO. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Relief of non-tension pneumothorax. Additional Normal Saline bolus. Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Target scene time is <10 minutes if trauma alert. \u2022 Criteria for chest decompression \u27a2 Absent or decreased breath sounds \u27a2 Tracheal deviation (late sign) \u27a2 Hypotension \u27a2 Respiratory distress \u27a2 Jugular venous distension (late sign) \u27a2 Difficult BVM ventilations after ET tube confirmation ; O2 saturation remains < 93% on 100% oxygen.",
  "adult/drowning-near-drowning": "90 Adult Trauma: Drowning/Near Drowning EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Remove wet clothing and cover with dry blankets. \u2022 Spinal motion restriction if indicated preferably before removal from water. \u2022 Cold water \u2013 treat per hypothermia protocol. \u2022 Cardiac arrest \u2013 treat per indicated cardiac, seizure, and trauma protocols. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation). EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. \u2022 Treat cardiac dysrhythmias per protocol. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC \u2022 Treat seizures per protocol Sodium Bicarbonate 1 mEq/kg IV/IO up to 100 mEq for suspected acidosis. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Resuscitation should be considered if submersion has been less than 2 hours, especially if water temperature is below 70 degrees. \u2022 Obtain the following history: \u27a2 Submersion time. \u27a2 Water temperature and depth. \u27a2 Clean or dirty water and any known pollutants. \u27a2 Mechanism of injury. \u27a2 Type of clothing and flotation device. \u27a2 Location last seen.",
  "adult/head-injuries": "91 Adult Trauma: Head Injuries EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Spinal motion restriction if indicated. \u2022 Penetrating objects should be stabilized \u2013 DO NOT REMOVE THEM. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation). EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. \u2022 Systolic blood pressure < 90 and tachycardia present administer 500 ml Normal Saline bolus. Repeat as necessary to maintain > 90 systolic blood pressure. Auscultate to ensure tolerance. Ondansetron (Zofran) for nausea 4 mg IV/IO. Repeat once in 5 minutes as needed. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC \u2022 Combative head injuries and/or seizures utilize one of the following: Midazolam 2 mg IV/IO or 5 mg IM/IN. Repeat as necessary to maximum of 10 mg. Lorazepam 1-2 mg IV/IO/IM/IN; repeat as necessary up to maximum of 6 mg in 5 minutes. \u2022 IV/IO do not exceed 2 mg/minute. \u2022 Must be diluted in equal amounts of Normal Saline to give IV/IO. \u2022 IM do not dilute, give injection deep into muscle mass. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Scene time target is 10 minutes. \u2022 Record vital signs every 5 minutes if patient is critical.",
  "adult/hemorrhage-control": "92 Adult Trauma: Hemorrhage Control Management EMR EMT \u2022 Correct circulation life threats with correct treatment procedures. \u25aa If hemorrhaging cannot be controlled by direct pressure, elevation or pressure points, apply tourniquet superior to hemorrhaging site. Distal pulses should be absent after application. Consider hemostatic agent application. \u2022 CAB and vital signs, (vitals taken every five minutes). \u2022 Spinal motion restriction if indicated. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor 4-Lead only may be applied and printed (no interpretation). EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Cardiac monitor \u2022 12-lead for suspected cardiac contusions. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO or up to 2000 ml bolus to maintain systolic BP >90. Assess lung sounds for tolerance. Pain control - see pain management protocol. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional Normal Saline bolus. Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Contact Medical Control as soon as possible. \u2022 Transport immediately to an appropriate Level 1 Trauma Center. \u2022 See patient transport policy to determine appropriate Trauma Center. \u2022 Tourniquets are only used for life threatening peripheral hemorrhages when all other means of hemorrhage control have been unsuccessful. \u2022 Tourniquets should only be removed by a physician in an appropriate hospital setting, preferably a level 1 trauma center.",
  "adult/isolated-trauma": "93 Adult Trauma: Isolated EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Stabilize fractures and stop bleeding. \u2022 Spinal motion restriction if indicated. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Cardiac monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation). EMR STOP EMT See pain management protocol for pain management. EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. See pain management protocol for pain management. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 All fractures should be stabilized. \u2022 Ensure that pulses, motor, and sensations are checked in extremities before, after, and whenever the patient is moved. \u2022 Traction splints are to be used only on mid shaft femur fractures.",
  "adult/multi-system-trauma": "94 Adult Trauma: Multi-Systems EMR EMT \u2022 Correct any life threats with correct treatment procedures. \u2022 CAB and vital signs (vitals taken every five minutes). \u2022 Spinal motion restriction if indicated. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Cardiac monitor (4-Lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation). EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac monitor. \u2022 12-lead for suspected cardiac contusions. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline @ TKO or up to 2000 ml bolus to maintain systolic BP > 90. Assess lung sounds for tolerance. Pain control - see pain management protocol. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC Tranexamic Acid- Adult (>40kg): Mix 1000mg (1 gram) of TXA in 100 mL of 0.9% Normal Saline; Infuse over10 minutes (5gtt/3seconds OR 25gtt/15seconds) o Any patient at high risk for ongoing massive traumatic hemorrhage and meeting one or more of the following criteria: o Systolic BP < 90mmHg, and/or physical shock appearance (confusion, AMS, cool extremities, etc.) o Heart rate >110bpm PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional Normal Saline bolus. Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Contact Medical Control as soon as possible. \u2022 Transport immediately to an appropriate Level 1 Trauma Center. \u2022 Ensure receiving facility is informed PTA of TXA administration \u2022 See patient transport policy to determine appropriate Trauma Center.",
  "adult/shock-hypoperfusion": "95 Adult Trauma: Shock/Hypoperfusion EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Cardiac monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation). EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac monitor. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline @ TKO or up to 2000 mL bolus to maintain systolic BP >90. Assess lung sounds for tolerance. Ondansetron (Zofran) for nausea 4 mg IV/IO. Repeat once in 5 minutes. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC Dopamine 2-20 mcg/kg/minute IV/IO infusion. o MED MATH: DOPAMINE: Add one vial (200mg/5mL) Dopamine to 120mL NS in a buretrol for a total of 200mg/125mL = 1600mcg/mL; Drip rate is figured same as with pre-mixed bag PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional Normal Saline bolus. Key Points/Considerations \u2022 For patients with hypoperfusion due to trauma, bleeding, vomiting, diarrhea, or sepsis, fluid bolus must be administered first. \u2022 Contact a physician if you suspect cardiogenic shock. \u2022 Contact receiving hospital early.",
  "adult/spinal-motion-restriction": "96 Adult Trauma: Spinal Motion Restriction EMR EMT AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Perform an appropriate trauma assessment. \u2022 Determine mechanism: \u27a2 Low Risk: (Requires all of the following) \u25aa Patient reliable and competent? o Normal level of alertness (GCS 15) o No evidence of clinical intoxication. o No distracting injury. o Able to communicate adequately. \u25aa Lack of point tenderness on palpation of entire length of spinous processes. \u25aa Lack of focal deficit (motor or sensory) on neuro exam. \u2022 No to any of the above -> Spinal Motion Restriction is required. \u27a2 High Risk: (Requires at least one of the following) \u25aa Ejection from a vehicle \u25aa Motorcycle crash >20 mph \u25aa Auto vs. pedestrian or bike at > 20 mph \u25aa Axial load to head \u25aa Fall from 3x patient\u2019s height \u2022 Yes to any of the above Spinal Motion Restriction is required. \u2022 Patient documentation must specifically include all of the above findings. EMR, EMT, AEMT, and PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Transport as soon as possible. \u2022 Penetrating trauma patients without neuro deficits do NOT require spinal motion restriction. \u2022 Long backboard or KED may still be used in patient extrication even if spinal motion restriction is not required by protocol, but should be removed as soon as possible.",
  "pediatric/pediatric-emergencies-vital-signs": "97 Pediatric Emergencies \u2022 IO access should be attempted if no peripheral access can be obtained after 1 attempt or 90 seconds in patients with cardiovascular collapse. \u2022 Protocols may be used fluidly. \u2022 Buretrol is required for all IVs that have fluids running on patients < 9 years of age. \u2022 Have a Broselow Pediatric Tape or similar device available to accurately determine the correct medication dosage. Deviate from color codes to match actual weight. At no time should the pediatric dosage exceed adult dosage. \u2022 Normal Vital Signs for Infants and Children: Age Respirations Pulse Systolic BP Newborn 30-60 100-180 >60 Infant (<1 year) 30-60 100-160 >60 Toddler (1-3 years) 24-40 90-150 >70 Preschooler (3-5 years) 22-34 80-140 >75 School-aged (6-8 years) 18-30 70-120 >80 From: American Academy of Pediatrics, Pediatric Education for Pre-hospital Professionals.",
  "pediatric/cpr-procedure": "99 Pediatric Cardiac Arrest: Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR) Indications: All pediatric cardiac arrest situations. Treat reversible causes concurrently per appropriate protocol CPR Quality \u2022 Push fast (110/min), allow complete recoil. \u2022 Pediatric/Neonate chest compressions should be >1/3rd depth of the anterior-posterior diameter of chest. \u2022 Minimize interruptions in chest compressions; interruptions should be infrequent and for no longer than 10 seconds. \u2022 Avoid excessive ventilation. Ventilate at a rate of 8-10 breaths per minute. \u2022 1-rescuer Pediatric/Neonate CPR: 30:2 compression-ventilation ratio (continuous compressions upon adequate resources) \u2022 2-rescuer Pediatric/Neonate CPR: 15:2 compression-ventilation ratio (continuous compressions upon adequate resources) \u2022 After advanced airway placement, perform continuous chest compressions and ventilate at 8-10 breaths per minute. \u2022 Rotate compressor every 220 or when compression quality decreases as indicated by Real CPR or capnography trending. Electrical Therapy \u2022 Biphasic defibrillation: Pediatric/Neonate 1st dose 2j/kg. All repeat doses 4j/kg. \u2022 AED settings are preset by manufacturer. Medication Therapy Normal Saline IV/IO : o Pediatric 20 ml/kg. May repeat once if needed Pediatric/Neonate: Epinephrine 0.01 mg/kg IV/IO every 3-5 minutes Pediatric/Neonate Pulseless VF/VT options: Magnesium 25-50 mg/kg IV/IO diluted into 10% solution; Max single dose 2 gm (Torsades De Pointes Only) Amiodarone 5 mg/kg IV/IO repeat once in 3-5 minutes. Lidocaine 1 mg/kg IV/IO may repeat once in 3-5 minutes. Consider Sodium BiCarb 1mEq/kg IV/IO after 10 minutes. Repeat once in 5 minutes 0.5 mEq/kg (Asystole, PEA,VF,VT) Start CPR & apply metronome Suction/clear airway PRN OPA BVM Ventilations @ 8-10 breaths per/min Utilize Zoll Real CPR Drug Therapy: IV/IO access Epinephrine every 3-5 minutes Amiodarone for refractory VF/VT Lidocaine for refractory VF/VT Magnesium for Torsades De Pointes Consider Advanced Airway Monitor ETCO2 with advanced airway",
  "pediatric/cardiac-arrest": "100 Pediatric Cardiac Arrest EMR EMT \u2022 Begin CPR and attach AED \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy via BVM. EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Determine and document rhythm in two leads. \u2022 Biphasic defibrillation: o First dose: 2j/kg. o Repeat doses: 4j/kg. Normal Saline IV/IO at 20mL/kg; May repeat once PRN Epinephrine 1:10,000 0.01 mg/kg IV/IO every 3-5 minutes; No more than 5 Epi total regardless of rhythm changes o Pediatric/Neonate Pulseless VF/VT options: Amiodarone 5 mg/kg IV/IO; repeat once in 3-5 minutes. Lidocaine 1 mg/kg IV/IO; repeat once in 3-5 minutes AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC Magnesium 25-50 mg/kg (50% solution) IV/IO diluted into 10% solution; Max single dose 2 gm (Torsades) MED MATH: Draw up 1Gm/2mL into 8mL NS = 1Gm/10mL = 1000mg/10mL; Example: If you have 20kg pt, dose is 500mg -> 5mL Consider Sodium BiCarb 1mEq/kg IV/IO after 10 minutes; Repeat once in 5 minutes 0.5 mEq/kg (Asystole/PEA/VT/VF). PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Early physician contact. \u2022 Confirm asystole in more than 1 lead \u2022 Perform CPR for up to 3 minutes between medication doses. \u2022 IO access should be attempted if no peripheral access can be obtained after 1 attempt or 90 seconds in patients with cardiovascular collapse \u2022 DEXTROSE MED MATH: For patients <40 kg: Dilute D50 to concentration of D25: Draw up 25 ml of D50 and dilute with normal saline to total volume of 50 ml (Equals 1 gm/2 ml) \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards. \u2022 Do not interrupt compressions for placement of an advanced airway. Avoid excessive ventilation. \u2022 Insert NG tube for gastric distension if needed. \u2022 Capnography to confirm and monitor ET tube placement and quality of CPR. \u27a2 Consider and treat causes of PEA: \u27a2 Hypoxemia (hyperventilate) \u27a2 Hypovolemia (fluid challenge 20 ml/kg bolus) \u27a2 Hypoglycemia (<60 mg/dL) (Dextrose) \u27a2 Hypothermia (warm) \u27a2 Hyperkalemia/Hydrogen Ion (acidosis) (NaHCO3 1mEq/kg with a max 100 mEq) \u27a2 Pulmonary Embolism (ventilate) \u27a2 Tension Pneumothorax (Needle decompression) \u27a2 Cardiac Tamponade (fluid challenge) \u27a2 Overdose (Opiate) (Narcan 0.1 mg/kg with a max single dose 2 mg)",
  "pediatric/management-post-resuscitation": "101 Pediatric Cardiac Arrest: Management/Post Resuscitation EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Continue high flow oxygen via non-rebreather or BVM as indicated. \u2022 Check blood glucose level and treat per diabetic protocol if indicated. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (No interpretation). EMR, EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Acquire 12-lead \u2022 IV/IO access: Normal saline bolus 20 ml/kg repeat as necessary to maintain a B/P of at least 90 systolic. Repeat if indicated. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC \u2022 Capnography monitoring if intubated. \u2022 Post resuscitation ventricular ectopy: watchful waiting is usually the most appropriate action while improved oxygenation takes effect and catecholamine levels return to normal. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional fluids. Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Contact Medical Control to tailor the treatment to the patient\u2019s specific needs. \u2022 Vital signs should be recorded every 5 minutes on critical patients. \u2022 Even mild hypotension should be avoided because it can impair recovery of cerebral function. \u2022 Symptomatic Bradycardia \u2013 Reassess ventilations and airway. If profound bradycardia persists after reassessment, consider Cardiac Dysrhythmia/Bradycardia Protocol. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards. \u2022 Monitor lung sounds when administering fluid. Do not fluid overload the patient.",
  "pediatric/termination-of-resuscitation": "102 Pediatric Cardiac Arrest: Termination of Resuscitation EMR EMT \u2022 Resuscitative efforts for patients in cardiac arrest should not be initiated if: o Patient presents with significant dependent lividity, rigor mortis, decomposition and/or injuries incompatible with life (such as decapitation). o There is a signed DNR order. \u2022 For all other patients in respiratory or cardiac arrest, the EMS provider MUST start BLS care, including defibrillation, and request a Type I assist if Type II. \u2022 All pediatric cardiac arrests from birth to 17 years that do not fall under the above code black criteria will be resuscitated for a minimum of 20 minutes on scene unless ROSC occurs. After 20 minutes of resuscitation and no ROSC the patient will be transported code blue to the nearest appropriate facility. EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Complete standing orders appropriate to presenting rhythm. \u2022 EXCEPTION: Parent(s) or legal guardian understand and agree to termination of efforts. \u2022 All \u201cTRAUMA\u201d pediatric cardiac arrest from birth to 17 y/o that do not meet Code Black criteria: o Will be assessed BLS/ALS AND o Basic airway maneuvers performed AND o Bilateral chest decompression(s) performed AND o Ages 0-1years: 22g IV needle, 2nd ICS mid-clavicular line o Ages 1-4years: 20g IV needle, 2nd ICS mid-clavicular line o Confirmation of asystole in 2 or more leads. \u25aa NO RESPONSE: Triage Code Black in the field and DO NOT transport. \u25aa RESPONSE: If the patient responds to efforts, continue with Code Blue Trauma protocol. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Attempt to obtain a copy of the DNR or advanced directive to be attached to the chart.",
  "pediatric/chest-pain": "103 Pediatric Cardiac: Chest Pain EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (No interpretation). EMR STOP EMT \u2022 Acquire 12-lead (No interpretation). EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Acquire 12-lead. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline TKO. Consider 20 ml/kg normal saline bolus if BP < 90mmHg (or hypotensive for age) \u2022 Follow appropriate protocol for rhythm changes. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional medications Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Notify receiving hospital as soon as possible if patient is a STEMI alert.",
  "pediatric/dysrhythmia-bradycardia": "104 Pediatric Cardiac: Cardiac Dysrhythmia/Bradycardia EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 If heart rate is < 60 bpm with poor perfusion despite effective ventilation with oxygen, initiate chest compressions w/ compression to ventilation ratio 15:2 or 30:2 if single provider. \u2022 Check blood glucose and treat hypoglycemia per protocol \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (No interpretation). EMR STOP EMT \u2022 Acquire 12-lead (no Interpretation) EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Acquire 12-lead EKG. \u2022 IV/IO access. \u2022 Normal Saline: Consider 20 ml/kg bolus AEMT Stop PARAMEDIC \u2022 Bradycardia: Persistent or responds only transiently: Epinephrine push-dose pressor: 0.01mg/kg (0.1mL/kg) every 3 minutes; TITRATE TO MAP >65mmHg; max single dose 10mg or 1mL; EXAMPLE: if you have 10kg pt, give 1mL MED MATH: Prepare 10mcg/mL (1:100,000) Epi by adding 1mL of 1:10,000 Epi (0.1mg/mL) to 9mL NS; Atropine 0.02 mg/kg IV/IO; minimum initial dose 0.1 mg, max initial dose 0.5mg; max total dose of 1 mg. o Give for increased vagal tone or cholinergic drug toxicity \u2022 Medication failure: Transcutaneous pacing starting @ 100 bpm or call physician for further orders. Consider sedation and/or pain control prior to pacing: Midazolam IV/IO 0.05mg/kg infused slowly over 1-2 minutes OR IM/IN 0.1mg/kg; Max single dose 5mg. Repeat as necessary up to a maximum of 10 mg. Morphine 0.1 mg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN, repeat in 3-5 minutes as necessary up to maximum 10 mg. Fentanyl 0.5-1 mcg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN with max initial dose 100mcg; Repeat in 5 min PRN at \u00bd initial dose, up to total dose 3mcg/kg Dilaudid 0.01-0.02 mg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN as necessary. Maximum of 0.5 mg. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional Fluids or Medications Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Early physician contact. \u2022 Newborn/Infant bradycardic if pulse less than 80 bpm; child over 1 year of age bradycardic if pulse less than 60 bpm. \u2022 Symptomatic includes poor systemic perfusion, hypotension, hypoxia, respiratory difficulty or altered level of consciousness \u2022 IO access should be attempted if no peripheral access can be obtained after 1 attempt or 90 seconds in patients with cardiovascular collapse \u2022 If bradycardia is due to increased vagal tone or primary AV block give atropine before giving epinephrine. \u2022 Do not treat asymptomatic bradycardia. Contact medical control. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards.",
  "pediatric/dysrhythmia-tachycardia-with-pulse": "105 Pediatric Cardiac: Dysrhythmia / Tachycardia with a Pulse EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor 4-Lead only may be applied and printed (No interpretation of ECG rhythm) EMR STOP EMT \u2022 12-lead (no interpretation) EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac Monitor. \u2022 Acquire 12-lead. \u2022 IV/IO access Normal Saline TKO. Normal Saline 20mL/kg bolus if systolic BP is <90 mmHg and if no signs and symptoms of fluid overload exist. \u2022 Identify and treat possible causes. \u2022 Unstable rhythms require synchronized cardioversion: \u2022 Narrow, Regular QRS: 1st dose 1J/kg; Repeat doses 2J/kg \u2022 Wide, Regular QRS: 1st dose 1J/kg; Repeat doses 2J/kg AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC \u2022 If patient is stable use medications first, then perform synchronized cardioversion if refractory to medications. \u2022 Narrow, Regular QRS (e.g. SVT): Adenosine 0.1mg/kg IVFP/IO w/ NS flush, max 6mg May repeat in 3 minutes at 0.2mg/kg IVFP/IO w/ NS flush, max 12mg \u2022 Narrow, Irregular QRS (e.g. A-Fib, A-Flutter, MAT): Amiodarone 5mg/kg IV/IO over 10 minutes, max 150mg/dose \u25aa MED MATH: Draw up 150mg Amiodarone into 10mL NS for 15mg/mL concentration; Example: if you have a 3kg pt, give 15mg/1mL at 0.1mL/min; 10kg pt give 50mg OR 3mL at 0.3mL/min. Hold onto the syringe plunger while infusing to prevent backflow; will need to be given with fluids to allow for flushing between doses. \u2022 Wide, Regular QRS (e.g. V-Tach, SVT/A-Fib/A-Flutter w/ aberrancy): Amiodarone 5mg/kg IV/IO over 10 min, max 150mg/dose \u2022 Wide, Irregular QRS (e.g. A-Fib w/ aberrancy, WPW w/ A-Fib, MAT, Polymorphic VT/Torsades de Pointes) Amiodarone 5mg/kg IV/IO over 10 min, max 150mg/dose \u2022 Consider sedation prior to cardioversion: Midazolam 0.2 mg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN infused slowly over 1-2 minutes; Max singled dose 5mg. Repeat as necessary up to a maximum of 10 mg. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Synchronized cardioversion of irregular rhythms Additional or alternative medication options o Adenosine for SVT w/ aberrancy Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Contact Medical Control as soon as possible. 106",
  "pediatric/newborn-resuscitation": "107 Pediatric: Newborn Resuscitation EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs \u2022 APGAR \u2022 CPR if indicated \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead EKG (No interpretation). EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Monitor EKG. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline bolus 20 ml/kg IV/IO as needed \u2022 Check blood glucose level and treat per diabetic protocol if indicated. \u2022 Strong cry, regular respiratory effort, good tone, term gestation -> skin-to-skin w/ mom and cover w/ dry linens \u2022 Weak cry, signs of respiratory distress, poor tone, preterm -> Position airway to sniffing position and clear as needed \u2022 Suction meconium with meconium aspirator and advanced airway. \u2022 HR >100bpm: \u2022 Monitor for central cyanosis; Provide blow-by oxygen as needed \u2022 Monitor signs of respiratory distress o Apneic or significant distress: BVM w/ room air at 40-60 breaths/min o Consider intubation \u2022 HR <100bpm: \u2022 BVM ventilations w/ room air at 40-60 breaths/min \u2022 If no improvement after 90 seconds: change to oxygen administration until HR normalizes \u2022 Consider ETI if ventilations are ineffective \u2022 HR <60bpm: \u2022 Ensure effective ventilations w/ supplementary oxygen and adequate chest rise \u2022 If no improvement after 30 seconds: o Initiate chest compressions w/ two-thumb-encircling-hands technique o Coordinate compressions w/ PPV (3:1 ratio; 90 compressions and 30 breaths every minute) o Consider ETI Epinephrine 1:10,000 dose 0.01mg/kg IV/IO AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional fluid bolus. Access of umbilical vein if unable to obtain venous or intraosseous access Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards. \u2022 Keep warm.",
  "pediatric/abdominal-pain": "109 Pediatric: Abdominal Pain EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Cardiac Monitor 4-Lead only may be applied and printed (No interpretation of ECG rhythm) EMR STOP EMT \u2022 12-lead (no interpretation) o Ondansetron (Zofran) for nausea 4mg PO may repeat x1 in 5 minutes. EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline @ TKO. Ondansetron (Zofran) for nausea 1 mg IV/IO/IM. May repeat once in 5 minutes. Morphine 0.1 mg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN, repeat in 3-5 minutes as necessary up to maximum 10 mg. Fentanyl 0.5-1 mcg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN with max initial dose 100mcg; Repeat in 5 min PRN at \u00bd initial dose, up to total dose 3mcg/kg Dilaudid 0.01-0.02 mg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN as necessary. Maximum of 0.5 mg. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC Toradol: <2yrs \u2013 0.25mg/kg; >2yrs \u2013 0.5mg/kg; Max dose 30mg PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Begin transport as soon as possible. \u2022 Transport patient in a position of comfort if possible. \u2022 Use caution palpating patients with possible cardiac involvement, i.e. aortic aneurysms. \u2022 Pediatrics must be > 4 years to administer Ondansetron. \u2022 SPO2 and cardiac monitoring required with any narcotic administration.",
  "pediatric/abuse-neglect": "110 Pediatric: Abuse / Neglect EMR EMT AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Initiate treatment as necessary. \u2022 If possible remove child from scene, transporting to hospital even if there is no medical reason for transport. \u2022 Allow a parent to be with the child during transport unless a suspicion of further trauma exists or the severity of injuries warrants the separation. \u2022 If parents refuse permission to transport, notify law enforcement to respond to scene. If patient is in immediate danger, let law enforcement handle scene. \u2022 AVOID ACCUSATIONS as this may delay transport. Adult with child may not be the abuser. \u2022 Never communicate via radio abuse suspicions. \u2022 Carefully document findings and report to physicians at the hospital. \u2022 Immediately contact the appropriate law enforcement agency to file a report. Make sure to include this in your documentation of the call. EMR, EMT, AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 EMS care providers are MANDATORY REPORTERS by law with the duty to notify authorities of suspected child abuse. Reports must also be submitted via DCF website. \u2022 Child abuse/neglect are widespread enough that nearly all EMTs and Paramedics will see these problems at some time. The first step in recognizing abuse or neglect is to accept that they exist and to learn the signs and symptoms: \u27a2 Noticeable injuries not mentioned in the history. \u27a2 History does not explain physical findings of the injury. \u27a2 Injuries with a pattern: o Bite marks o Cigarette Burns o Rope (circumferential or linear marks at the ankles or wrists) o Loop scars (doubled-over electrical wire or telephone cord) o Belt marks o Etc. \u27a2 Injuries to genital or perineal areas. \u27a2 Multiple repetitive unexplained injuries. \u27a2 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape.",
  "pediatric/altered-mental-status-unresponsive": "111 Pediatric: Altered Mental Status/Unresponsive EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Spinal motion restriction if trauma suspected. \u2022 Check blood glucose level and treat per diabetic protocol if indicated. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation) EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Acquire 12-lead. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline TKO. D25 Dextrose 0.5 gm/kg IV/IO per Pediatric Diabetic protocol followed with 20 ml/kg normal saline infusion up to 100 ml. DEXTROSE MED MATH: For patients <40 kg: Dilute D50 to concentration of D25: Draw up 25 ml of D50 and dilute with normal saline to total volume of 50 ml (Equals 1 gm/2 ml) Narcan 0.1mg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN per the Overdose protocol. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Consult Medical Control as soon as possible. \u2022 IO access should be attempted if no peripheral access can be obtained after 1 attempts or 90 seconds in patients with cardiovascular collapse. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards. \u2022 Potential causes of altered mental status: \u27a2 A \u2013 Alcohol \u27a2 E \u2013 Epilepsy, Endocrine, Electrolytes \u27a2 I \u2013 Insulin \u27a2 O \u2013 Opiates and other drugs \u27a2 U \u2013 Uremia \u27a2 T \u2013 Temperature, Trauma \u27a2 I \u2013 Infection \u27a2 P \u2013 Psychogenic, Poisoning \u27a2 S \u2013 Shock, Stroke, Space-occupying lesion, Subarachnoid hemorrhage \u2022 Questions to consider: \u27a2 Is patient newborn of narcotic using mother? \u27a2 What was patient doing prior to this? \u27a2 Has it happened before?",
  "pediatric/anaphylaxis-allergic-reaction": "112 Pediatric Medical: Anaphylaxis/ Allergic Reactions EMR CAB and vital signs. Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. o Consider ventilator assistance for severe dyspnea or hypoventilation. \u2022 Determine cause of reaction, time of exposure, and amount of substance patient was exposed to if possible. o Remove patient from source or source from patient if still present. o For Moderate Allergic Reaction (e.g., hives, itching, redness, stable vitals) administer: Diphenhydramine 9kg(22lbs)-20kg (43lbs)- 12.5mg PO (1 tablet) Diphenhydramine 21kg(55lbs) 36kg(80lbs) \u2013 25mg PO (2 tablets) For Anaphylaxis administer: Epinephrine 25kg (55lbs) 1:1,000 dose 0.15 mg IM used first for shock states; Repeat x1 in 5 minutes, as necessary. EMR STOP EMT Albuterol 2.5 mg in 3 mL (unit dose) + Ipratropium 0.5 mg in 2.5 mL (unit dose) mixed together via nebulizer for wheezing. May repeat, as necessary. EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Acquire 12-lead. \u2022 IV/IO access. \u2022 Normal Saline TKO. If hypotensive 20 ml/kg fluid bolus. Repeat one time to maintain BP > 90. Diphenhydramine 1 mg/kg IV/IO/IM AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC Epinephrine 1:10,000 dose 0.01 mg/kg IV/IO; max 0.3 mg. Methylprednisolone (Solu-Medrol) 1 mg/kg IV/IO push. Epinephrine Push Dose Pressor 0.01mg/kg = 0.1mL/kg 1:100,000 every 3-5 minutes, max single dose 10mcg o MED MATH: Prepare 10mcg/mL (1:100,000) Epi by adding 1mL of 1:10,000 Epi (0.1mg/mL) to 9mL NS; Example: 10kg pt, give 1mL PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 S/S of allergic reaction: gradual onset, mild flushing, rash, or hives, mild bronchoconstriction, and normal mental status. \u2022 S/S of anaphylaxis: sudden onset, severe flushing, rash or hives, severe bronchoconstriction, laryngospasm, anxiety, sense of impending doom, may decrease to confusion, then unconsciousness. \u2022 Severe reactions may develop within 2-20 minutes following exposure. \u2022 Consider concurrent use of Pediatric Respiratory Distress protocol.",
  "pediatric/dehydration": "113 Pediatric: Dehydration EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation) EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Acquire 12-lead \u2022 IV/IO access \u2022 With signs and symptoms of dehydration present: o Patient must have tachycardia > 140 bpm and at least one additional sign and symptom: \u27a2 Dry mucous membranes \u27a2 Skin tinting \u27a2 Hypotension \u27a2 Decreased LOC related to suspected dehydration \u27a2 High Fever \u27a2 Severe vomiting or diarrhea \u27a2 Heat related emergency \u27a2 Pointed and peaked T-waves Normal Saline 20 ml/kg bolus; may repeat x1. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Monitor lung sounds closely throughout bolus. \u2022 Monitor SPO2. \u2022 Excessive rapid fluid replacement can cause an imbalance in electrolytes and other lab values. This may increase the patient\u2019s stay at the hospital. With this said minor dehydration will not be treated in the field. The above criteria must be met to treat dehydration in the field.",
  "pediatric/diabetic-emergencies": "114 Pediatric: Diabetic Emergencies EMR EMT \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy. \u2022 Check blood glucose level and treat per diabetic protocol if indicated. \u2022 Cardiac monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed if indicated (no interpretation) If blood glucose is known to be low and patient is able to self-administer and swallow on command, give oral glucose one unit dose (24 gms). EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Cardiac monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline @ TKO \u2022 Hyperglycemia: Blood glucose above 250 mg/dl and ONLY if signs of dehydration are present: Normal Saline bolus 20 ml/kg IV/IO. \u2022 Hypoglycemia: Blood glucose <60 administer Dextrose D25 at 0.5mg/kg IV/IO followed with 20 ml/kg normal saline infusion up to 100 ml DEXTROSE MED MATH: Dilute D50 to a concentration of D25: draw up 25 ml of D50 and dilute with normal saline to total volume of 50 ml. Equals 12.5gms/50mL or 0.25gm/mL If unable to obtain IV, administer Glucagon: o 0.5 mg IM for Broselow colors gray-white. o 1 mg IM for Broselow colors blue-green. If IM Glucagon unsuccessful establish IO for Dextrose. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional Dextrose Additional Normal Saline bolus Key Points/Considerations \u2022 DKA has slow onset lasting 12-24 hours. S/S include polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia, warm, dry skin and mucous membranes, nausea/vomiting abdominal pain, tachycardia, Kussmaul\u2019s respirations, fruity breath odor, decreased mental function, or coma. \u2022 If the patient\u2019s parent/guardian wishes to refuse transport: \u27a2 Must be present. \u27a2 Patient is alert. \u27a2 Blood Glucose greater than 60 mg/dl. \u27a2 No other complaints or complications. \u2022 Instructed to give complex carbohydrates, contact physician, and to recheck blood glucose regularly. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards.",
  "pediatric/nausea-and-or-vomiting": "115 Pediatric: Nausea and/or Vomiting EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non- rebreather. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead. (no interpretation) EMR STOP EMT Ondansetron (Zofran) ODT 4mg PO. Repeat once in 10 minutes if needed. EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Cardiac monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline @ TKO. Ondansetron (Zofran) 1 mg IV/IO/IM for nausea as necessary. Repeat one time in 5 minutes. \u2022 Consider dehydration protocol. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Pediatric patient must be > 4 years to receive Ondansetron. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards.",
  "pediatric/overdose-toxic-exposure": "116 Pediatric: Overdose or Toxic Exposure EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non- rebreather. \u2022 Determine what was taken, when, and how much. \u2022 Contact Poison Control Center at 1-800-222-1222. \u2022 Check blood glucose level. Treat per Diabetic protocol if indicated. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead. (no interpretation) \u2022 Suspected Opiate Overdose - If symptomatic (unresponsive with respirations less than 10/min or signs of inadequate perfusion, pinpoint pupils): Naloxone (Narcan) 1mg IM repeat in 5 minutes as necessary to a maximum of 2 mg. Cardiac Arrest Dose: 1mg IM repeat in 5 minutes as necessary to a maximum of 2 mg. EMR STOP EMT Activated Charcoal 1-2 gms/kg PO (Maximum dose 50 gms). EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Acquire 12-lead if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access. \u2022 Normal Saline @ TKO. Symptomatic opiate overdose: Naloxone (Narcan) 0.1 mg/kg IV/IO/IN every 5 minutes as necessary; not to exceed 6 mg. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC Organophosphate poisoning: Atropine 0.05 mg/kg IV/IO every 3 \u2013 5 minutes until secretions dry. Dystonic reaction: Diphenhydramine (Benadryl) 1 mg/kg IV/IO/IM. TCA overdose: Sodium Bicarbonate 1 mEq/kg IV/IO over 2 minutes. Beta blocker OD: Glucagon o 0.5 mg IM for Broselow colors gray-white o 1 mg IM for Broselow colors blue-green. Calcium channel blocker OD: Glucagon o 0.5 mg IM for Broselow colors gray-white o 1 mg IM for Broselow colors blue-green. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Repeat Activated Charcoal. Additional antidotes as needed. 117 Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Dystonic reaction is uncontrolled contractions of face, neck, or tongue. \u2022 Monitor patients with TCA overdose closely for rapid changes and deterioration in changes in their condition. \u2022 Obtain and give to ER staff all meds/containers found. \u2022 Obtain what meds were taken (pill bottles), number of pills taken, time of ingestion and if vomiting occurred. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards. \u2022 See following page for further information. \u2022 S/S of Phenothiazine overdose: \u27a2 Tardive Dyskinesia (involuntary movements of the facial muscles and tongue) and acute dystonic reactions. \u2022 S/S of TCA overdose: \u27a2 Widened QRS >0.10 \u27a2 Tachycardia >120 bpm \u27a2 Hypotension \u2022 S/S of Organophosphate poisoning: \u27a2 Muscle fasciculation \u27a2 Diarrhea \u27a2 Wheezing \u27a2 Abdominal cramping \u27a2 Salivation \u27a2 Seizures \u27a2 Altered mental status \u27a2 Pinpoint pupils \u27a2 DUMBELS: Diarrhea, urination, miosis, bradycardia, bronchospasm, bronchorrhea, emesis, lacrimation, salivation, sweating, secretions, seizures. \u27a2 SLUDGE: Salivation, lacrimation, urination, diarrhea, GI cramps, emesis. \u2022 S/S of atropinization: \u27a2 Papillary dilation \u27a2 Tachycardia \u27a2 Flushing \u27a2 Diminished diaphoresis \u27a2 Drying of secretions \u2022 S/S of Opiate overdose: \u27a2 Respiratory <10 \u27a2 Pinpoint pupils \u27a2 Loss of consciousness Calcium Blockers TCA Beta Blockers Organophosphates Verapamil Amitriptyline Inderal Disulfoton Cardiazem Amoxapine Lopressor Tetraethyl Pyrophosphate Nifedipine Clomipramine Tenormin Parathion Nicardipine Desipramine Corgard Phosdrin Nimodipine Dothiepin Blocadren Phorate Felodipine Imipramine Cartrol Doumaphos Amlodipine Lofepramine Visken Chlorpytigos Bepridil Maprotiline Sectral Trichlorfon Flunarizine Nortriptyline Normodyne Ronnel Isradipine Protriptyline Betapace Diazinon Nitrendipine Trimipramine Zebeta Malathion List is not all inclusive.",
  "pediatric/pain-management": "118 Pediatric: Pain Management EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non- rebreather. \u2022 Monitor SPO2. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation) EMR STOP EMT Acetaminophen Oral Solution 15mg/kg PO or: (ONLY FOR PTS >60 DAYS OLD) Weight (lbs) Weight (kg) mL of Acetaminophen <6 <2.7 Not indicated 6 - 11 2.7 - 5.0 1.25 12 - 17 5.1 - 7.7 2.5 18 - 24 7.8 - 10.9 3.75 25 - 49 11.0 - 22.3 5 \u226550 \u226522.3 10 EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Acquire 12-lead. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline @ TKO. Ondansetron (Zofran) 1 mg IV/IO/IM for nausea (> 4 years). Repeat one time in 5 minutes. Morphine 0.1 mg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN up to 5mg; repeat as necessary one time in 5 \u2013 10 minutes; maximum 10 mg Fentanyl 0.5-1 mcg/kg IV/IO/IN/IM w/ max initial dose 100mcg; Repeat in 5 min PRN at \u00bd initial dose, up to total dose of 3mcg/kg Dilaudid 0.01 \u2013 0.02 mg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN; repeat as necessary once up to a max of 1 mg. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC Toradol: <2yrs \u2013 0.25mg/kg IV; >2yrs \u2013 0.50mg/kg IV; Max dose 30mg \u25aa May be used alone or in conjunction with narcotic PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional pain medication doses Key Points/Considerations \u2022 For patients with: \u27a2 Severe burns without hemodynamic compromise. \u27a2 Isolated extremity injuries such as fractures or dislocations with severe pain. \u27a2 Suspected hip fractures should be treated as extremity injuries. \u27a2 Shoulder injuries should be treated as extremity injuries. \u2022 Contraindications to standing order pain management: altered mental status secondary to a head injury, hypoventilation, hypoperfusion. \u2022 Pediatrics must be > 6 months to administer Dilaudid. 119 \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards. \u2022 SPO2 and cardiac monitoring required with any narcotic administration. \u2022 Acetaminophen: Ensure pt has not had total dose of >15mg/kg within the last 4 hours (e.g. Tylenol, acetaminophen-containing medications such as cold medicines or narcotics)",
  "pediatric/respiratory-emergencies": "120 Pediatric: Respiratory Emergencies EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non- rebreather. \u2022 Monitor SPO2. \u2022 Determine if patient has been given their own asthma medications. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation) EMR STOP EMT Albuterol 2.5 mg in 3 ml (unit dose) + Ipratropium 0.5 mg in 2.5 ml (unit dose) mixed together, via nebulizer; repeat as necessary. EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac Monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access. \u2022 Normal Saline TKO. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC Methylprednisolone (Solu-Medrol) 1 mg/kg IV/IO. Epinephrine 1:1000 dose 0.01 mg/kg IM max 0.3 mg. May repeat x 1. Magnesium Sulfate 25 - 50 mg/kg IV IO diluted to a 10% solution over 1 \u2013 2 minutes MED MATH: Using 10mL syringe, draw up 1gm/2mL Mag and then add 8mL NS for 100mg/mL and then use 3- way stopcock to transfer desired dose from this syringe; Example: 5kg pt, draw/give 125mg = 1.25mL; 20kg pt, draw/give 5mL = 500mg Severe Croup Treatment: o < 4 years: 2.5 mg (2.5mL) Epinephrine 1:1000 diluted to 3 ml with normal saline. o > 4 years: 4 mg (4mL) Epinephrine 1:1000 nebulized. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Position patient sitting or in position of comfort. \u2022 Absence of breath sounds can be indicative of status asthmaticus. Be prepared for imminent respiratory arrest. \u2022 Presence of wheezing is not only an indicator of Asthma/COPD. Consider pulmonary edema, pneumonia, upper airway obstruction, anaphylaxis, and acute allergic bronchospasm. \u2022 Epinephrine should be reserved for those who are unable to generate adequate tidal volume to deliver aerosolized drug to the bronchial tree. \u2022 Draft nebulizer with BVM \u2022 Humidified O2 for croup/epiglottitis \u2022 Invasive airway maneuvers with upper airway disorders (croup, foreign body, epiglottitis) should only be attempted when in respiratory arrest. \u2022 Monitor heart rate closely. When administering Albuterol contact a physician if heart rate increases to >180 bpm. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards.",
  "pediatric/seizures": "121 Pediatric: Seizures EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Check blood glucose level refer to Pediatric Diabetic protocol. \u2022 Cardiac monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation) EMR STOP EMT FEVER >100.4\u00b0F/38\u00b0C: Acetaminophen Oral Solution 15mg/kg PO or: (ONLY FOR PTS >60 DAYS OLD) Weight (lbs) Weight (kg) mL of Acetaminophen <6 <2.7 Not indicated 6 - 11 2.7 - 5.0 1.25 12 - 17 5.1 - 7.7 2.5 18 - 24 7.8 - 10.9 3.75 25 - 49 11.0 - 22.3 5 \u226550 \u226522.3 10 EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline @ TKO. \u2022 Administer one of the following within 3 minutes of seizure activity: Lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg IVSP/IO/IN/IM. Repeat x 1 to a maximum of 4 mg. \u27a2 IV/IO do not exceed 2 mg/min; Must be diluted w/ equal amount of normal saline \u27a2 IM do not dilute, give injection deep into muscle mass. Midazolam IV/IO 0.05mg/kg infused slowly over 1-2 minutes OR IM/IN 0.1mg/kg; Max single dose 5mg. Repeat as necessary up to a maximum of 10 mg. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional Midazolam. Additional Lorazepam. Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Do not delay medication administration. Consider IN or IM prior to IV access. \u2022 Protect the patient and EMS crew from injury during the seizure. \u2022 Paramedic may assist the patient\u2019s family or caregivers with administration of rectal Valium (Diastat) if available. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards. \u2022 Fever in infants <60 days is NOT to be treated w/ Acetaminophen by EMS \u2022 Acetaminophen: Ensure pt has not had total dose of >15mg/kg within the last 4 hours (e.g. Tylenol, acetaminophen-containing medications such as cold medicines or narcotics)",
  "pediatric/sepsis": "122 Pediatric Medical: Sepsis EMR \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Check blood glucose level if equipped. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (No interpretation). \u2022 Obtain temperature EMR STOP EMT \u2022 Acquire 12-lead (No interpretation). \u2022 Apply/monitor EtCO2 capnography. FEVER >100.4\u00b0F/38\u00b0C: Acetaminophen oral solution 15mg/kg PO or: (ONLY FOR PTS >60 DAYS OLD) Weight (lbs) Weight (kg) mL of Acetaminophen <6 <2.7 Not indicated 6 - 11 2.7 - 5.0 1.25 12 - 17 5.1 - 7.7 2.5 18 - 24 7.8 - 10.9 3.75 25 - 49 11.0 - 22.3 5 \u226550 \u226522.3 10 EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Acquire/Interpret 12-lead ECG. \u2022 Large-bore IV access or IO if unable to establish IV access w/in 90 seconds. Normal Saline infusion at rate of 30mL/kg/hr in boluses using pressure bag. o May repeat 3x up to 60mL/kg, ensuring no pulmonary edema. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Focus on rapid intervention, IV hydration, and early notification of concern for potential sepsis pt. \u2022 Concern for any new or worsening infection can include: reported fever, shaking chills, sweatiness, new cough, difficulty or less than usual urination, unexplained or new AMS, flushed skin, pallor, new rash or mottling. \u2022 Vital signs should be obtained every 5 minutes. \u2022 Fever in infants <60 days is NOT to be treated w/ Acetaminophen by EMS \u2022 Acetaminophen: Ensure pt has not had total dose of >15mg/kg within the last 4 hours (e.g. Tylenol, acetaminophen-containing medications such as cold medicines or narcotics)",
  "pediatric/stroke": "123 Pediatric: Stroke EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Determine at what point signs and symptoms appeared. \u2022 If onset of symptoms is less than 5 hours, notify appropriate stroke center and transport immediately. \u2022 If onset is within 24 hours patient should be taken to a stroke center as a non-stroke alert patient. \u2022 Check blood glucose. If out of normal limits treat per Diabetic Protocol. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation) EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Secure airway as needed. \u2022 Acquire 12-lead. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline @ TKO \u2022 If tachycardic and blood pressure is < 90 systolic, 10 ml/kg normal saline IV/IO, to maintain blood pressure above 90. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Contact hospital as soon as possible to give Stroke Alert. \u2022 Limit scene time. \u2022 Repeat vitals every 5 minutes if patient meets stroke criteria. \u2022 All strokes that are within the 6 hour or \u201cwake-up stroke\u201d time limit are Code Red. Transport regular traffic unless hemodynamically unstable. o Cincinnati Stroke Scale \u25aa Speech \u2013 repeat: \u201cYou can\u2019t teach an old dog new tricks\u201d. Correct words and no slurring. \u25aa Facial Droop \u2013 Have patient smile. \u25aa Arm Drift \u2013 Have patient close their eyes and have them hold their arms out straight for 10 seconds without unequal movement or drifting.",
  "pediatric/carbon-monoxide-poisoning": "125 Pediatric: Carbon Monoxide Poisoning EMR \u2022 Remove patient from the source. \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and high flow oxygen at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non- rebreather. \u2022 Consider ventilatory assistance for severe dyspnea or hypoventilation. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation) EMR STOP EMT \u2022 Obtain carbon monoxide reading. EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Cardiac Monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline @ TKO \u2022 Treat seizures and cardiac dysrhythmias per protocols. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Pulse oximeter reading will be inaccurate. \u2022 All CO poisoning patients will be transported to Wesley Medical Center for hyperbaric oxygen treatment. \u2022 S/S: dull headache, weakness, dizziness, N/V, shortness of breath, confusion, blurred vision, loss of consciousness \u2022 All patients with suspected CO poisoning are required to have high flow oxygen initiated and maintained. \u2022 *100 percent oxygen reduces the elimination half-life of CO to 74 minutes, i.e. a SPCO 10% in theory would decrease to 5% after 74 minutes of oxygen @ 15 lpm via NRB. **In pregnant patients the CO levels of the fetus is double of the mother\u2019s",
  "pediatric/drowning": "126 Pediatric: Drowning EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Remove wet clothing and cover with dry blankets. \u2022 Spinal motion restriction if indicated preferably before removal from water. \u2022 Cold water \u2013 treat per hypothermia protocol. \u2022 Cardiac arrest \u2013 treat per indicated Cardiac, Seizure, and Trauma protocols. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation) EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Cardiac Monitor if indicated. IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 No longer requires a trauma alert unless there is associated traumatic MOI involved (e.g. boating accident, diving, significant fall, etc.) \u2022 Resuscitation should be considered if submersion has been less than 2 hours, especially if water temperature below 70 degrees. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape. \u2022 Obtain the following history: \u27a2 Submersion time. \u27a2 Water temperature and depth. \u27a2 Clean or dirty water and any known pollutants. \u27a2 Mechanism of injury. \u27a2 Type of clothing and flotation device. \u27a2 Location last seen.",
  "pediatric/hyperthermia": "127 Pediatric: Hyperthermia EMR EMT \u2022 Remove from heat source. \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Obtain temperature. o > 103 degrees cool patient with ice packs to the groin, neck, and axilla. Cool water and fanning of the patient is also effective. Remove when body temperature is 100 degrees. \u2022 Remove clothing. \u2022 Airway management and oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non- rebreather. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation) EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Cardiac monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access. \u2022 Normal Saline @ TKO or 20 ml/kg to maintain BP > 90, with hypovolemia and hemodynamic compromise. Assess breath sounds for tolerance. \u2022 Treat seizures per Seizure protocol. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Use caution with cooling - this can cause shivering or hypothermia. \u2022 Vigorous fluid administration may produce pulmonary edema. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards.",
  "pediatric/hypothermia": "128 Pediatric: Hypothermia EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. (Assess for 30-45 seconds) \u2022 CPR if indicated. \u2022 AED if indicated \u2013 one attempt only. \u2022 If not in cardiac arrest remove patient from environment avoiding any rough handling. Remove any wet clothing, cover with blankets, and place the heater on high. Patient must be kept warm. \u2022 Obtain temperature. \u2022 Airway management and oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non- rebreather. \u2022 Check blood glucose level. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation) EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Cardiac monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline @ TKO. o Warm IV fluids at 20 ml/kg. \u2022 If temperature is below 86 degrees F, CPR and one defibrillation @ 2 j/kg if indicated until temp is above 86 degrees. \u2022 Follow Cardiac Arrest protocol if patient is pulseless, apneic, and temperature is above 86 degrees F, but lengthen intervals of IV medications until temperature is above 93 degrees F. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Prevent further heat loss in patients < 86 degrees; temperatures > 93 degrees may be re-warmed. \u2022 Intubation should be avoided in the severely hypothermic patient with spontaneous respirations unless urgently needed using extreme caution. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards.",
  "pediatric/burns": "130 Pediatric: Burns EMR EMT \u2022 Stop the burning process. o Remove necessary clothing leaving all stuck clothing. o Remove rings, bracelets, and constricting bands. o Flush briefly with cool water if still burning. (chemical burns only) \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non- rebreather. \u2022 Maintain body temperature. \u2022 Chemical burns: o Irrigate with copious amounts of water or saline for 20 minutes. EXCEPTION: Phosphorus and Lime Powder. o Brush off dry powder chemicals before flushing with water. \u2022 Decontaminate all patients exposed to hazardous materials before entering the ambulance. \u2022 Elevate extremity in position of function to minimize edema monitoring neuro/vascular status. \u2022 Treat for shock and hypothermia. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation) EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Cardiac monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline: o 5 year old and younger \u2013 125 ml/hour o 6-13 year old \u2013 250 ml/hour o 14+ year old \u2013 500 ml/hour Morphine 0.1 mg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN up to 5 mg. Repeat as necessary every 3-5 minutes to a maximum of 20 mg. Fentanyl 0.5-1mcg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN with max initial dose 100mcg; Repeat in 5min PRN at \u00bd initial dose, up to total dose of 3mcg/kg Dilaudid 0.01-0.02 mg/kg IV/IO; Max single dose 0.25mg; Max total dose 4mg AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Begin transport as soon as possible to Via Christi on St. Francis. \u2022 Consider burn alert criteria. \u2022 Use Rule of Nine\u2019s when determining total body surface. \u2022 Remove patient from exposure without compromising crew safety. \u2022 Infuse warm IV fluids if available. \u2022 Electrical Burns: consider spinal immobilization and assess for extremity fractures. \u2022 Electrical, Flame, or scald burns should be covered with dry sterile sheets and dressings. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards.",
  "pediatric/chest-abdominal-injuries": "131 Pediatric Trauma: Chest / Abdominal Injuries EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. o Record vital signs every 5 minutes on critical patients. \u2022 Stabilize life-threatening wounds (Control hemorrhage, cover sucking chest wounds, etc.) \u2022 Airway management and oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non- rebreather. \u2022 Spinal motion restriction if indicated. \u2022 Moist sterile dressing to abdominal evisceration. \u2022 Penetrating objects should be stabilized \u2013 DO NOT REMOVE THEM. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation) EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access. \u2022 Normal Saline @ TKO or: 20 ml/kg bolus IV/IO maintaining a systolic B/P > 90. \u2022 Treat pain per pain management protocol. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC \u2022 Chest injury with tension pneumothorax. o Chest decompression with available pneumothorax kit. \u27a2 Needle decompression with 14 ga IV catheter if pneumothorax kit is unavailable. \u27a2 Age 0-1years: 22ga IV catheter \u27a2 Age 1-4years: 20ga IV catheter \u27a2 Code Blue Trauma \u2013 (bilateral) prior to termination of arrest. \u2022 Consider sedation in conscious patient prior to decompression. Midazolam IV/IO 0.05mg/kg infused slowly over 1-2 minutes OR IM/IN 0.1mg/kg; Max single dose 5mg. Repeat as necessary up to a maximum of 10 mg. \u2022 Crush injury for an extended time > 45 minutes. Consider Sodium Bicarbonate 1 mEq/kg. IV/IO. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Relief of non-tension pneumothorax. Additional Normal Saline bolus. Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Begin transport as soon as possible. \u2022 Target scene time is <10 minutes if trauma alert. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards. \u2022 Criteria for chest decompression. \u27a2 Absent or decreased breath sounds \u27a2 Tracheal deviation \u27a2 Hypotension \u27a2 Severe respiratory distress \u27a2 Jugular venous distension \u27a2 Difficult BVM ventilations after ET tube confirmation. O2 saturation remains >93% on 100% oxygen.",
  "pediatric/head-injuries": "132 Pediatric Trauma: Head Injuries EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non- rebreather. \u2022 Spinal motion restriction if indicated. \u2022 Penetrating objects should be stabilized \u2013 DO NOT REMOVE THEM. \u2022 Consider soft restraints for combative patients. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation) EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT \u2022 Cardiac monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal Saline @ TKO: 20 ml/kg bolus maintaining a systolic B/P of 90. AEMT STOP PARAMEDIC \u2022 Combative head injury: Lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg IV/IO/IM/IN up to 2 mg repeat X 1 in 5 minutes. IV/IO dilute in equal amount normal saline. o IV/IO do not exceed 2mg/min o Must be diluted in equal amount of normal saline to give IV/IO; IM do not dilute, give deep into muscle mass Midazolam IV/IO 0.05mg/kg infused slowly over 1-2 minutes OR IM/IN 0.1mg/kg; Max single dose 5mg. Repeat as necessary up to a maximum of 10 mg. PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Scene time target is 10 minutes. \u2022 Record vital signs every 5 minutes if patient is critical. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards.",
  "pediatric/isolated-trauma": "133 Pediatric: Isolated Trauma EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Stabilize fractures and stop bleeding. \u2022 Spinal motion restriction if indicated. \u2022 Airway management and oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non- rebreather. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation) EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Cardiac monitor if indicated. IV/IO access Normal Saline @ TKO. \u2022 See pain management protocol for pain management. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 All fractures should be stabilized. \u2022 Ensure that pulses, motor, and sensations are checked in extremities before, after, and whenever the patient is moved. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards.",
  "pediatric/multi-systems-trauma": "134 Pediatric: Multi-Systems Trauma EMR EMT \u2022 Correct any life threats with correct treatment procedures. \u2022 CAB and vital signs taken every five minutes. \u2022 Spinal motion restriction if indicated. \u2022 Airway management and oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non- rebreather. \u2022 Acquire 4-lead (no interpretation) EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Cardiac monitor. \u2022 12-lead for suspected cardiac contusions. \u2022 IV/IO access. Normal saline @ TKO or: 20 ml/kg bolus maintaining a systolic B/P of 90. \u2022 Pain control: see pain management protocol. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Additional normal saline bolus. Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Contact Medical Control as soon as possible. \u2022 Transport immediately to Wesley Medical Center. \u2022 Tourniquets are only used for life threatening peripheral hemorrhages when all other means of hemorrhage control have been unsuccessful. \u2022 Tourniquets should only be removed by a physician in an appropriate hospital setting, preferably a level 1 trauma center.",
  "pediatric/shock-hypoperfusion": "135 Pediatric: Shock/Hypoperfusion EMR EMT \u2022 CAB and vital signs. \u2022 Airway management and appropriate oxygen therapy at 2-6 LPM via nasal cannula or when appropriate 12-15 LPM via non-rebreather. \u2022 Cardiac monitor (4-lead only) may be applied and printed (no interpretation) EMR AND EMT STOP AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Cardiac Monitor if indicated. \u2022 IV/IO access. \u2022 Normal Saline: 20 ml/kg bolus maintaining a systolic B/P > 90. AEMT AND PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Contact a physician if you suspect cardiogenic shock. \u2022 Diagnostic criteria for hypoperfusion includes: capillary refill time > 2 seconds, cool, clammy or mottled skin, inability to recognize parents, restlessness, listlessness, tachycardia, tachypnea, systolic BP less than 70 mmHg (2 years and older) or systolic BP less than 60 mmHg (less than 2 years old). \u2022 Contact receiving hospital early. \u2022 Heart rate is more important than blood pressure if less than 3 years old. \u2022 Refer to Broselow Pediatric Emergency Tape/Pediatric Reference Cards.",
  "pediatric/spinal-motion-restriction": "136 Pediatric: Spinal Motion Restriction EMR EMT AEMT PARAMEDIC \u2022 Perform an appropriate trauma assessment. \u2022 Determine mechanism: \u27a2 Low Risk: (Requires all of the following) \u25aa Patient reliable and competent? o Normal level of alertness (GCS 15) o No evidence of clinical intoxication. o No distracting injury. o Able to communicate adequately. \u25aa Lack of point tenderness on palpation of entire length of spinous processes. \u25aa Lack of focal deficit (motor or sensory) on neuro exam. \u2022 No to any of the above Spinal Motion Restriction is required. \u27a2 High Risk: (Requires at least one of the following) \u25aa Ejection from a vehicle \u25aa Motorcycle crash >20 mph \u25aa Auto vs. pedestrian or bike at > 20 mph \u25aa Axial load to head \u25aa Fall from 3x patient\u2019s height \u2022 Yes to any of the above Spinal Motion Restriction is required. \u2022 Patient documentation must specifically include all of the above findings. EMR, EMT, AEMT, and PARAMEDIC STOP PHYSICIAN OPTIONS Key Points/Considerations \u2022 Transport as soon as possible. \u2022 Penetrating trauma patients without neuro deficits do NOT require spinal motion restriction. \u2022 Long backboard, pediatric board, or KED may still be used in patient extrication even if spinal motion restriction is not required by protocol but should be removed as soon as possible.",
};
